{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788249149086,"sessionID":"ses_fa40f0460ffeYpKGnJPSwDwXVy","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_d3f9da9c63f24192841ab6c7","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"source venv/bin/activate && python << 'EOF'\nimport csv, collections\n\n# BR-Football seasons per tournament\nwith open('data/kaggle/BR-Football-Dataset.csv', encoding='utf-8') as f:\n    r = csv.DictReader(f)\n    by_t = collections.defaultdict(set)\n    for row in r:\n        by_t[row['tournament']].add(row['date'][:4])\nfor t, s in by_t.items():\n    print(t, sorted(s))\n\n# Libertadores NA season rows\nprint()\nwith open('data/kaggle/Libertadores_Matches.csv', encoding='utf-8') as f:\n    r = csv.DictReader(f)\n    na = [row for row in r if row['season']=='NA']\n    print('NA season rows:', len(na))\n    for row in na[:5]: print(row)\nEOF"},"output":"Copa do Brasil ['2014', '2015', '2016', '2017', '2018', '2019', '2020', '2021', '2022', '2023']\nSerie A ['2014', '2015', '2016', '2017', '2018', '2019', '2020', '2021', '2022', '2023']\nSerie B ['2014', '2015', '2016', '2017', '2018', '2019', '2020', '2021', '2022', '2023']\nSerie C ['2014', '2015', '2016', '2017', '2018', '2019', '2020', '2021', '2022', '2023']\n\nNA season rows: 1\n{'datetime': 'NA', 'home_team': 'Flamengo', 'away_team': 'Athletico', 'home_goal': '-', 'away_goal': '-', 'season': 'NA', 'stage': 'final'}\n","metadata":{"output":"Copa do Brasil ['2014', '2015', '2016', '2017', '2018', '2019', '2020', '2021', '2022', '2023']\nSerie A ['2014', '2015', '2016', '2017', '2018', '2019', '2020', '2021', '2022', '2023']\nSerie B ['2014', '2015', '2016', '2017', '2018', '2019', '2020', '2021', '2022', '2023']\nSerie C ['2014', '2015', '2016', '2017', '2018', '2019', '2020', '2021', '2022', '2023']\n\nNA season rows: 1\n{'datetime': 'NA', 'home_team': 'Flamengo', 'away_team': 'Athletico', 'home_goal': '-', 'away_goal': '-', 'season': 'NA', 'stage': 'final'}\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"source venv/bin/activate && python << 'EOF'\nimport csv, collections\n\n# BR-Football seasons per tournament\nwith open('data/kaggle/BR-Football-Dataset.csv', encoding='utf-8') as f:\n    r = csv.DictReader(f)\n    by_t = collections.defaultdict(set)\n    for row in r:\n        by_t[row['tournament']].add(row['date'][:4])\nfor t, s in by_t.items():\n    print(t, sorted(s))\n\n# Libertadores NA season rows\nprint()\nwith open('data/kaggle/Libertadores_Matches.csv', encoding='utf-8') as f:\n    r = csv.DictReader(f)\n    na = [row for row in r if row['season']=='NA']\n    print('NA season rows:', len(na))\n    for row in na[:5]: print(row)\nEOF","time":{"start":1788249149083,"end":1788249149085}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now I have a good understanding of the data. Let me plan the implementation:\n\n**Architecture:**\n\n```\n.\n├── server.py                  # MCP server entry point (stdio)\n├── soccer_mcp/\n│   ├── __init__.py\n│   ├── models.py              # dataclasses: Match, Player, TeamRecord, etc.\n│   ├── normalize.py           # team name normalization (accents, suffixes, aliases)\n│   ├── data_loader.py         # loads all 6 CSVs into memory\n│   ├── queries/\n│   │   ├── __init__.py\n│   │   ├── matches.py         # match search (team, date, competition, season)\n│   │   ├── teams.py           # team stats, records, head-to-head\n│   │   ├── players.py         # player search (name, nationality, club)\n│   │   ├── competitions.py    # standings, schedules\n│   │   └── stats.py           # aggregates: biggest wins, averages, home/away\n│   └── formatting.py          # response formatting\n├── tests/\n│   ├── test_match_queries.py  # BDD GWT-style tests\n│   ├── test_team_queries.py\n│   ├── test_player_queries.py\n│   ├── test_competition_queries.py\n│   ├── test_stats.py\n│   ├── test_normalization.py\n│   └── test_server.py         # MCP server tests\n├── requirements.txt\n├── pytest.ini or pyproject\n└── README.md (update)\n```\n\n**Key design decisions:**\n\n1. **MCP server**: Use the official `mcp` Python SDK (FastMCP) — stdio transport. Tools:\n   - `search_matches(team, opponent, competition, season, date_from, date_to, stage, limit)`\n   - `head_to_head(team_a, team_b)`\n   - `team_stats(team, season, competition, venue)` \n   - `team_vs_team_compare` (maybe part of head_to_head)\n   - `search_players(name, nationality, club, position, min_overall, limit)`\n   - `top_players(club/nationality, limit)`\n   - `standings(competition, season)`\n   - `competition_info(competition)` — seasons available, teams, etc.\n   - `biggest_wins(competition, season, limit)`\n   - `average_goals(competition, season)`\n   - `home_away_split(...)` or team performance analysis\n   - `derbies(season)` — known rivalries\n   - `last_match_between(team_a, team_b)`\n   - `list_competitions()`\n   - `list_teams(competition, season)` \n   - `find_team(name)` — resolve team name variations\n   \n   Maybe also a knowledge-graph-ish tool? The spec says \"provides a knowledge graph interface\". Hmm — \"MCP server that provides a knowledge graph interface for Brazilian soccer data\". I think tools that let the LLM traverse entities (teams, players, matches, competitions) as a graph is the intent. I can add tools `find_team`, `find_player`, `find_competition`, `find_match` that resolve entities and return references, plus relationship queries (`head_to_head`, `team_players`, `competition_teams`, `team_matches`). That's a reasonable \"knowledge graph interface\" without requiring an actual graph DB. \n\n   The prompts.txt mentions Neo4j as one variant of the prompt (\"Use Neo4j\") but our instruction says \"implement everything TASK.md asks for\" — TASK.md doesn't mandate Neo4j. The prompts.txt is explicitly \"ignore this file\". So a pure-Python in-memory implementation with the official MCP SDK is fine. I'll build an in-memory graph layer (nodes/edges) to satisfy the \"knowledge graph interface\" wording.\n\n2. **Team name normalization**: \n   - Strip accents, lowercase\n   - Remove state suffixes (\"-SP\", \" - RJ\", \"(RJ)\")\n   - Handle known aliases: \"Sport Club Corinthians Paulista\" -> \"corinthians\"; \"América FC (Minas Gerais)\" -> \"america mg\"; \"Ceará Sporting Club\" -> \"ceara\"; \"Sport Club do Recife\" -> \"sport\"; \"Athletico-PR\"/\"Atlético-PR\"/\"Athletico Paranaense\" — hmm, Atlético-PR and Athletico-PR are the same club (renamed 2019). \"América-MG\" vs \"America FC Natal\" vs \"América-RN\".\n   - Remove common suffix words: \"EC\", \"FC\", \"Clube\", \"Sport Club\", \"Esporte Clube\", \"Futebol Clube\"? Careful — \"Sport-PE\" is a specific team (Sport Club do Recife), and \"Sport Recife\" in BR-Football. Normalizing \"Sport Club do Recife\" by removing \"club\", \"do\", \"recife\" would break it. So suffix removal should be limited to a curated alias table + state suffix stripping + accent folding.\n   \n   Strategy:\n   - Canonical form: lowercase, accent-stripped, state suffix removed, punctuation collapsed.\n   - Plus an explicit alias map for known variants:\n     - \"sport club corinthians paulista\" -> \"corinthians\"\n     - \"ceara sporting club\" -> \"ceara\"\n     - \"sport club do recife\" -> \"sport\"\n     - \"america fc (minas gerais)\" -> \"america mg\" (careful — \"América-MG\" normalizes to \"america mg\" after suffix strip? \"América-MG\" -> strip accents -> \"america-mg\" -> remove state suffix \"-mg\" -> \"america\". Hmm, that collides with América-RN -> \"america\". So state suffixes matter for disambiguation. Better approach: keep the state code as part of canonical name when present in the source: \"america mg\", \"america rn\". But \"America FC Natal\" -> contains \"natal\"... alias map: \"america fc natal\" -> \"america rn\". \"America RN\" -> \"america rn\". OK.\n     - Actually simpler: canonical name = base name + optional state. For matching, exact canonical match first; then fuzzy fallback (prefix/substring/token overlap). E.g., query \"Flamengo\" matches \"flamengo rj\" (from \"Flamengo-RJ\") by base-name token match.\n   \n   Let me design: \n   - `normalize(name)` -> canonical: strip accents, lowercase, remove text in parentheses, remove trailing state suffix pattern `-XX` or ` - XX` (2-letter UF), collapse spaces.\n   - Build a registry of all teams from all datasets with their canonical forms and variants. When searching for \"Palmeiras\", normalize to \"palmeiras\" and match any registry entry whose canonical base (state stripped) == \"palmeiras\" OR whose alias set contains it.\n   - Alias table for special cases (corinthians full name, sport recife, america mg, atletico/athletico paranaense, athletico-pr vs atletico-pr, \"atletico-mg\" vs \"atletico mineiro\" (FIFA club), \"gremio\" vs \"gremio fbpa\"? etc.)\n\n3. **Matches**: unify into a common Match dataclass:\n   - date (ISO), home_team (canonical), away_team, home_goals, away_goals, competition (brasileirao_serie_a, copa_do_brasil, libertadores, serie_b, serie_c), season, round/stage, stadium (from historical), plus optional stats (corners, shots, attacks) from BR-Football-Dataset.\n   - Dedup consideration: BR-Football-Dataset overlaps with Brasileirao_Matches / Brazilian_Cup_Matches for Serie A/Copa do Brasil 2020-2023ish. If both loaded, head-to-head would double count. Hmm. The spec says all 6 files must be loadable and queryable, and cross-file queries work. I'll keep a `source` field on each match, and allow competition queries to filter by source. For head-to-head/stats, I should pick a primary source per competition to avoid duplication: \n     - Serie A: Brasileirao_Matches (2012-2022) + novo_campeonato_brasileiro (2003-2019) + BR-Football (2020-2023, includes Serie A). Overlap: Brasileirao_Matches 2020-2022 vs BR-Football 2020-2023 Serie A; novo 2003-2019 overlaps with Brasileirao 2012-2019.\n     \n     Hmm, this is getting complicated. Approach: each match gets a `source` and `competition`. Queries can specify `competition` and optionally `source`. For standings and head-to-head, I'll dedupe by default: prefer the \"richest\" source? Or prefer the primary source per competition:\n     - For \"Serie A\" (Brasileirão): sources = ['brasileirao_matches' (2012-2022), 'novo_campeonato' (2003-2019), 'br_football' (2020-2023 Serie A)]. To avoid double counting a season, choose per (competition, season) a single source — preference: brasileirao_matches > br_football > novo? Wait: for Serie A 2012-2019, both brasileirao_matches and novo have data. For 2020-2022, both brasileirao_matches and br_football. For 2023, only br_football. For 2003-2011, only novo.\n     \n     I'll implement dedup policy: within a (competition, season) group, use only one source, in priority order: for serie_a: ['brasileirao', 'novo', 'br_football']? Hmm wait — actually let me reconsider: do the two sources for Serie A 2012-2019 (brasileirao_matches and novo) contain the same matches? Both are full seasons of Serie A, so yes, essentially the same matches. Priority: brasileirao_matches (cleaner ISO dates, state suffixes) then br_football (has extended stats) then novo (historical only).\n     \n     Actually, extended stats (corners/shots) only exist in br_football. Maybe default source priority should be: br_football first for coverage 2014-2023? But br_football Serie A starts... let me check the date range per tournament/season in BR-Football-Dataset. Actually let me check which seasons BR-Football has for Serie A.\n     \n     Let me not over-think: the guiding principle: **avoid double-counting the same match** in aggregated stats. I'll dedupe at the query level by preferring a deterministic source order per competition, and I'll expose a `source` filter parameter so users can query any specific file. For \"find all matches\" I'll return matches from all sources but mark duplicates? Hmm, that could return 2-3 copies of the same fixture.\n     \n     Cleaner: at load time, build a \"deduplicated match set\" for default queries: key = (date, home, away) roughly, but dates can differ between sources for the same fixture (postponed/recorded differently)... Actually for the same fixture, date should be the same. Key = (competition, season, home_canonical, away_canonical, date)? If the two sources disagree on date, we'd count both. Let me check the actual overlap empirically before deciding.\n\n   Let me check: Serie A 2019 in brasileirao_matches vs novo vs br_football.\n\n4. **Standings**: compute from matches: 3 points win, 1 draw. For Brasileirão (double round-robin). For Copa do Brasil / Libertadores (knockout), standings don't make sense — but the spec asks \"Who won the 2019 Brasileirão?\" and \"Show the 2018 Copa Libertadores bracket\". I can compute the Libertadores champion from the final(s). Note: Libertadores finals are two-legged; the dataset's \"final\" stage has 15 matches over 2013-2022 (some years may have single or two matches + the \"NA\" season?). Let me check.\n\n5. **Player queries**: straightforward on fifa_data.csv. Name search (case-insensitive substring, accent-insensitive), nationality filter, club filter (normalized club matching with team registry!), position filter, sort by overall. Cross-file: club matching links FIFA clubs to match-data teams.\n\n6. **Testing**: BDD GWT-structured pytest. Use classes named `TestX` with docstrings in Gherkin, or pytest-bdd? pytest-bdd isn't installed; I can install it, but writing GWT-style structured tests with Given/When/Then via fixtures and names is fine and dependency-free. The prompt variant mentioned \"BDD GWT structured PyTest\" — I'll structure tests with Given/When/Then in docstrings and method names like `test_...`. Maybe I'll write a tiny GWT helper (given/when/then functions) to make it explicit. Let me keep it simple: use pytest with Gherkin docstrings and clear arrange/act/assert sections. Also add tests for the MCP server itself using the mcp client (in-memory) to verify tools work end-to-end.\n\n7. **requirements.txt**: mcp, pytest (already in venv but document).\n\nNow, about MCP server implementation using the official SDK v2.1.1. Let me check the FastMCP API for 2.1.1. The modern API:\n\n```python\nfrom mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP\n\nmcp = FastMCP(\"brazilian-soccer\")\n\n@mcp.tool()\ndef search_matches(...) -> str:\n    ...\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    mcp.run()  # stdio\n```\n\nOr with the newer low-level Server API. FastMCP is simplest. In mcp 2.x, `from mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP` should still work. Let me verify quickly.\n\nFor testing the server, I can use `mcp.client.session.ClientSession` with `mcp.client.stdio` or better: FastMCP supports in-memory testing via `mcp.shared.memory.create_connected_server_and_client_session` — in newer versions there's `ClientSession(server)`. Let me check what's available in mcp 2.1.1.\n\nAlso the \"knowledge graph interface\": I'll add tools:\n- `find_team(name)` — resolve a team to canonical entity + metadata (states, competitions played, seasons, name variants)\n- `find_player(name)` \n- `list_competitions()`\n- `find_competition(name)` \n\nPlus relationship tools (team_matches, team_players, head_to_head, standings...). That forms the graph: Team -[played_in]-> Competition, Team -[played]-> Match, Player -[plays_for]-> Club(Team).\n\nLet me now check data specifics: Libertadores finals, BR-Football Serie A seasons, overlap between sources, and unusual values (e.g. \"NA\" season in Libertadores; missing goals?).","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05bf48e5c001711HAZrdJaEeEc","sessionID":"ses_fa40f0460ffeYpKGnJPSwDwXVy","messageID":"msg_05bf2e585001MYTbe2YC9SFSE9"}}
