OF'\nimport csv\ndef teams(path, *cols):\n    s = set()\n    with open(path, encoding='utf-8') as f:\n        for row in csv.DictReader(f):\n            for c in cols: s.add(row[c])\n    return s\n\nlib = teams('data/kaggle/Libertadores_Matches.csv','home_team','away_team')\ncup = teams('data/kaggle/Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv','home_team','away_team')\nbr  = teams('data/kaggle/Brasileirao_Matches.csv','home_team','away_team')\nprint('BR teams (%d):' % len(br))\nprint(sorted(br))\nEOF","time":{"start":1788249196281,"end":1788249196283}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Very informative:\n- Brasileirao_Matches 2019 has 343 (unique home/away pairs, missing some due to name differences?) — wait br19=343 vs nv19=380. Serie A 2019 had 380 matches (20 teams). The difference: \"atletico\" vs \"athletico\" — in Brasileirao_Matches, the team might be \"Athletico-PR\" while others \"Atlético-GO\", and base() strips the state so \"athletico-pr\" -> \"athletico\" and \"atletico-go\" -> \"atletico\", causing collisions (\"atletico\",\"atletico\" pair). Also note br∩nv=306 not 343 — because in novo, Athletico-PR is spelled \"Athletico-PR\" while in brasileirao it's... hmm, br19-bf shows \"atletico\" — so in Brasileirao_Matches, Athletico-PR appears as \"Atlético-PR\"? and Atlético-GO as \"Atlético-GO\"? Both normalize to \"atletico\" → collision → set loses pairs.\n\nThis confirms the need for careful normalization: **keep the state code as part of the canonical identity** to disambiguate (atlético-mg vs atlético-go vs athletico-pr vs atlético-pr are all different clubs; athletico-pr == atlético-pr historically, but atlético-go is different).\n\nSo canonical names should retain the state when known:\n- \"Palmeiras-SP\" -> \"palmeiras sp\"? or keep a separate base+state tuple. But then \"Flamengo\" (Libertadores file, no suffix) wouldn't match \"Flamengo-RJ\". \n\nDesign: each team has:\n- `canonical_id`: base name + optional state, e.g