tória\" → Vitória-BA, \"Paraná\" → Paraná-PR, \"Chapecoense\" → Chapecoense-SC, \"Bahia\" → Bahia-BA, \"Ceará Sporting Club\" → Ceará-CE, \"Sport Club do Recife\" → Sport-PE, \"América FC (Minas Gerais)\" → América-MG, \"Atlético Mineiro\" → Atlético-MG, \"Atlético Paranaense\" → Athletico-PR, \"Fluminense\" → Fluminense-RJ.\n\nCanonical ID scheme: \"base state\" where base is the shortest common form. Let me define canonical ids like \"flamengo rj\", \"atletico mg\", \"atletico pr\" (merging athletico), \"atletico go\", \"america mg\", \"america rn\", \"botafogo rj\", \"botafogo sp\", \"sport pe\", etc. Display names: \"Flamengo\", \"Atlético-MG\", \"Athletico-PR\", \"América-MG\"... I'll keep a display name preference: use the most common raw variant, prettified.\n\nMatching for queries: user says \"Flamengo\" → candidates: \"flamengo rj\" (major) and \"flamengo pi\" (minor, Copa do Brasil minnow). Resolution strategy: exact base match with unique state → if only one team has that base → use it. If multiple → prefer the one with more matches in the dataset (Flamengo-RJ wins overwhelmingly), but report ambiguity. I'll implement `resolve_team(query, allow_ambiguous=False)` that returns best match + alternatives. For match counts, Flamengo-RJ has hundreds; Flamengo-PI a handful. Default: pick the biggest; note alternatives in response.\n\nNow the dedup question for overlapping matches across sources. Let me quantify overlaps:\n- Serie A: brasileirao (2012-2022, 380/yr except 2020-2021 which had 35?), novo (2003-2019, 380/yr... note 2003-2005 had different formats — 2003: 46 games? whatever), br_football (2014-2023, 380/yr). Wait — br19 (brasileirao 2019) = 343 unique pairs but should be 380. The name collision: \"Atletico-GO\"→\"atletico\" and \"Atletico-PR\"→... wait no — in Brasileirao_Matches it's \"Atletico-PR\" (2019 spelled \"Atletico-PR\"?) and \"Atletico-GO\" — hmm 2019 Serie A didn't have Atletico-GO. The collision in my quick test was because my base() stripped state suffixes — \"Atletico-MG\" → \"atletico\", \"Atletico-PR\" → \"atletico\". So pairs like (atletico, x) collide when MG and PR both play x. That's why br19=343 unique pairs < 380. With proper base+state ids this won't be an issue.\n\nSo with correct canonicalization, Serie A 2019: brasileirao=380, novo=380, br_football=380 — the same 380 fixtures three times. For stats/standings/head-to-head I must use a single source per (competition, season). Source priority for Serie A:\n- I'd say: ['brasileirao_matches', 'br_football', 'novo_campeonato'] — wait, but br_football has extended stats (corners etc.) and covers 2014-2023 including 2023 which brasileirao doesn't. For standings, brasileirao (2012-2022) + br_football (2023) + novo (2003-2011). For 2012-2019: brasileirao vs novo — both fine; brasileirao has exact datetime; novo has Arena (stadium). Priority: brasileirao first.\n- For Serie B/C: only br_football. \n- Copa do Brasil: brazilian_cup (2012-2021) + br_football (2014-2023). Overlap 2014-2021! Priority: brazilian_cup (it's the dedicated file, rounds numbered). Hmm, but br_football has corners/stats. Default priority: brazilian_cup first; source param lets users pick br_football.\n- Libertadores: only libertadores file. \n\nDedup approach: `matches(competition=..., season=..., source=None)`: if source not given, pick the default source for that (competition, season) via priority, ensuring exactly one match per fixture. I'll precompute `DEFAULT_SOURCE[(comp, season)]` at load time. Additionally dedupe within a source by (home, away, date) key just in case.\n\nWait, one more check — do brazilian_cup and br_football Copa do Brasil have the same fixtures? 2019 Copa do Brasil: 1,337 total cup rows across 2012-2021. br_football Copa do Brasil 2014-2023. Let me spot check counts per season quickly. Also check Libertadores finals format (two legs) and the 'NA' row (Flamengo vs Athletico final — that's the 2022 Libertadores final second leg? Flamengo beat Athletico in 2022 final. The row has datetime NA and goals '-'. Actually the 2022 final: Athletico 0-1 Flamengo (first leg, in Paraná? no wait — the dataset probably has \"2022-10-26\" somewhere...). Let me check Libertadores 2022 final rows.\n\nAlso for standings for Copa do Brasil/Libertadores — knockout competitions; I'll provide \"champion\" determination: for Libertadores final stage matches, aggregate score; for Copa do Brasil, final = round 8? (rounds 1-8 with 14 matches in round 8 — two-legged final → 7 finals? hmm 2012-2021 = 10 seasons but 14 matches in round 8 — some years single-match finals at New Jersey? 2019? no... The Copa do Brasil final has been two-legged; 14 matches over 10 seasons — some years had 2 matches, some 1? e.g. 2020 final single match? Let me not over-engineer: champion determination for Copa do Brasil via round==max round per season, aggregate goals (with penalty info unavailable — if aggregate tied, declare \"decided on penalties (not in data)\" and pick... hmm. If tied on aggregate we can't know the winner. I'll report both finalists and the aggregate, noting penalty winner unknown when tied.)\n\nActually — let me focus. The spec's competition queries: \"Standings by season (calculated from match results)\" — primarily for Brasileirão. \"Who won the 2019 Brasileirão?\" → standings → champion. \"Show the 2018 Copa Libertadores bracket\" → I can list knockout-stage matches by round. \"Which teams were relegated in 2020?\" → bottom 4 of Serie A standings. Good.\n\nNow — number of sample questions to answer: ≥20. I'll include a `SAMPLE_QUESTIONS` list in README and ensure the tools cover them.\n\n**MCP tools** (final list):\n1. `list_competitions()` — competitions with season coverage, match counts, sources.\n2. `find_team(name)` — resolve team entity: canonical id, display name, state, name variants seen, competitions/seasons played, match counts. (knowledge-graph node lookup)\n3. `list_teams(competition, season)` — teams in a competition/season.\n4. `search_matches(team=None, opponent=None, competition=None, season=None, stage=None, date_from=None, date_to=None, limit=20, source=None)` — flexible match search.\n5. `head_to_head(team_a, team_b, competition=None, season=None)` — H2H record + recent matches.\n6. `team_stats(team, competition=None, season=None, venue=None)` — W/D/L, GF/GA, home/away split.\n7. `compare_teams(team_a, team_b, competition=None, season=None)` — side-by-side stats + H2H.\n8. `search_players(name=None, nationality=None, club=None, position=None, min_overall=None, max_age=None, limit=20)` — player search.\n9. `top_players(nationality=None, club=None, position=None, limit=10, attribute=None)` — top-rated players (attribute could be any skill column like 'Finishing').\n10. `standings(competition, season)` — computed table (points, W/D/L, GF/GA, GD); for knockout comps, returns finals/champion info instead.\n11. `champion(competition, season)` — winner determination (league → top of table; cup → final match(es)).\n12. `biggest_wins(competition=None, season=None, team=None, limit=10)` — largest margins.\n13. `average_goals(competition=None, season=None, team=None)` — avg goals/match, home win %, etc.\n14. `derbies(season=None, competition=None)` — matches between known rival pairs (Fla-Flu, Grenal, Choque-Rei, Clássico dos Milhões, Majestoso, etc.)\n15. `team_competitions(team)` — which competitions & seasons a team played in (cross-file).\n16. `team_players(team)` — cross-file: players at that club in FIFA data.\n17. `last_match(team, opponent=None)` — most recent match of a team (or between two teams).\n18. `knowledge_graph_overview()`? Maybe overkill. Skip.\n19. `find_player(name)` — single player detail lookup (like \"Who is Gabriel Barbosa?\").\n\nThat's plenty. Each returns formatted text (markdown-ish) per the spec's answer formats, since MCP tool results are for LLM consumption. But for testability, I'll separate the query layer (returns dataclasses/dicts) from formatting (returns strings). Tools = thin wrappers.\n\n**Performance**: load all CSVs once at startup (module-level lazy singleton). ~25k rows total — fast.\n\n**Testing**: pytest with GWT/BDD structure. I'll write a lightweight GWT framework:\n\n```python\nclass Given:\n    ...arrange helpers...\n```\n\nActually simplest robust approach: pytest tests with Given/When/Then sections in docstrings and clear structure, since installing pytest-bdd adds fragility. But \"BDD GWT structured PyTest\" from the sample prompt — I can honor it with a small `bdd.py` helper module providing `given/when/then` context-object steps. Let me create `tests/bdd.py`:\n\n```python\nclass GWT:\n    def given(self, description, fn=None): ...\n```\n\nHmm, let me design a clean minimal GWT helper:\n\n```python\nclass Scenario:\n    def __init__(self, name): self.name = name; self.ctx = {}\n    def given(self, key, value, description): self.ctx[key] = value; return self\n    def when(self, key, fn, description): self.ctx[key] = fn(self.ctx); return self\n    def then(self, fn, description): fn(self.ctx)\n```\n\nAnd tests like:\n\n```python\ndef test_find_matches_between_two_teams():\n    scenario = Scenario(\"Find matches between two teams\")\n    (scenario\n        .given(\"the match data is loaded\", data=load_data())\n        .when(\"I search for matches between 'Flamengo' and 'Fluminense'\",\n              result=lambda ctx: search_matches(ctx['data'], team='Flamengo', opponent='Fluminense'))\n        .then(\"I should receive a list of matches\", assert_fn=...))\n```\n\nThat's decent but a bit ceremonious. Alternative: keep Gherkin docstrings + plain pytest with fixtures named given_/when_/then_. I think a small explicit GWT helper is actually nice and testable, and directly satisfies \"BDD GWT structured PyTest\". I'll write it so failures raise informative assertion errors.\n\n**Server tests**: use `mcp` client session in-memory. In mcp 2.x: \n\n```python\nfrom mcp import ClientSession, StdioServerParameters\nfrom mcp.client.stdio import stdio_client\n```\n\nspawning server.py as subprocess. That requires the venv python — in tests, use sys.executable. That works: `StdioServerParameters(command=sys.executable, args=[\"server.py\"])`. And data dir resolution relative to repo root. This gives a true end-to-end test of the MCP server (list tools, call tools).\n\nAdditionally, unit tests for loader/normalization/queries.\n\n**Structure** (single package):\n\n```\nsoccer_mcp/\n  __init__.py\n  gwt.py            # tiny BDD Given-When-Then harness\n  normalize.py\n  data_loader.py\n  model.py          # dataclasses\n  knowledge.py      # team registry / graph relations\n  queries.py        # all query functions (matches, teams, players, comps, stats)\n  formatting.py\nserver.py\ntests/\n  conftest.py\n  test_bdd_match_queries.py\n  test_bdd_team_queries.py\n  test_bdd_player_queries.py\n  test_bdd_competition_queries.py\n  test_bdd_stats.py\n  test_bdd_normalization.py\n  test_mcp_server.py\nrequirements.txt\nREADME.md\n```\n\nThe task says \"Write all code files to the current directory\" — files in cwd (subdirectories are fine, they're still \"in the current directory\" project). Also \"Maintain a detailed context block comment at the start of every code file\" — that's from the *sample* prompt in prompts.txt which I'm told to ignore, but TASK.md itself doesn't require it. I'll include reasonable module docstrings anyway (good practice, and harmless).\n\n**Now details:**\n\n*normalize.py*:\n- `strip_accents(s)`\n- `canonical_team(raw, state_hint=None) -> (base, state)`:\n  - NFKD normalize, strip combining marks, casefold\n  - remove parenthetical content: `re.sub(r'\\(.*?\\)', ' ', s)`\n  - replace punctuation (.,',-) with space? careful: \"guarani-sp\" → \"guarani sp\"; \"a.b.c.\" → \"a b c\" → collapse → \"abc\"! \"a.b.c.\" with dots replaced by space gives \"a b c\" ≠ \"abc\". Hmm. \"A.b.c. - RN\" → I want \"abc rn\". If I remove dots entirely (no space): \"a.b.c.\" → \"abc\". \"c.r.b.\" → \"crb\". \"s.francisco\" → \"sfrancisco\". But \"guarani-sp\" → \"guaranisp\" — bad! Solution: state suffix extraction FIRST (using regex on the raw-ish string: `[ -]\\s*-?\\s*([A-Z]{2})$` case-insensitive), THEN remove dots/periods without space, THEN replace remaining hyphens with space.\n  \n  Order of operations:\n  1. strip accents, casefold\n  2. remove parentheses content\n  3. extract trailing state: regex `(?:^|\\s|[-–])\\s*([a-z]{2})\\s*$` — but careful: \"Sampaio Correa - MA\" → state \"ma\", base \"sampaio correa\". \"Vasco da Gama - RJ\" → \"vasco da gama\" + \"rj\". \"Botafogo - RJ\" → \"botafogo\"+\"rj\". \"Botafogo PB\" (BR-Football, no dash) → need to catch trailing 2-letter token even without dash? \"America MG\" → base \"america\", state \"mg\". But \"Rio Branco AC\" → \"rio branco\"+\"ac\". Risky: a team whose real name ends in 2 letters... e.g. \"EC\" endings? \"operario ferroviario esporte c\" ends with \"c\" (single letter). Two-letter endings that are states but part of name? I can't think of a Brazilian club whose name legitimately ends with a 2-letter state-like token that ISN'T the state. E.g. \"Serra F. C. - ES\" → after state strip \"serra fc\" → then remove dots → \"serra fc\" → hmm \"F. C.\" → dots removed → \"fc\"? \"serra f c\" vs \"serra fc\"? Dots-removed-no-space: \"serra fc\"? \"F. C.\" → \"FC\" → token \"fc\". OK good: dots removed, then it's \"serra fc\".\n     But what about bare \"Santos\"? no state. BR-Football \"Santos\" → state unknown → need alias: \"santos\" → \"santos sp\" via registry merging (the famous club). I'll handle state-less names via an explicit resolution table: STATE_HINTS = {'santos': 'sp', 'flamengo': 'rj', 'gremio': 'rs', ...} for the well-known clubs, applied when state is absent AND the base is famous. But \"Santos - AP\" exists in Cup... when state present, use it. When absent (\"Santos\" in BR-Football/Libertadores/FIFA), default to the famous one: Santos-SP. Since BR-Football also has \"Santos AP\" explicitly, the bare \"Santos\" must mean Santos-SP. \n  4. base cleanup: remove '.' (no space), replace '-' and other punct with space, collapse whitespace.\n  5. alias folding via table: e.g. \"atletico mineiro\"→\"atletico\" (with MG), \"athletico paranaense\"/\"atletico paranaense\"→\"atletico\" (with PR), \"america fc natal\"→\"america\" (RN), \"flamengo do piaui\"→\"flamengo\" (PI), \"sport club do recife\"→\"sport\", \"ceara sporting club\"→\"ceara\", \"sport club corinthians paulista\"→\"corinthians\", \"boavista sport club\"→\"boavista\", \"ser caxias\"→\"caxias\", \"aquidauanense futebol clube\"→\"aquidauanense\", \"parnahyba sc\"→\"parnahyba\", \"operario ferroviario esporte c\"→\"operario ferroroviario\"... hmm wait, \"Operário - PR\" — is Operário-PR the same as Operário Ferroviário-PR? Yes: Operário Ferroviário Esporte Clube, Ponta Grossa PR. But BR-Football has \"Operario PR\" AND \"Operario Ferroviario Esporte C - PR\"? BR-Football list shows 'Operario PR' (no 'Operario Ferroviario' — the latter is in the Cup file). Merge \"operario ferroviario esporte c\" → \"operario\" only when state==PR. But note \"Operario MS\" (Operário FC de Campo Grande) vs Cup's \"Operário - MS\" — consistent. OK.\n     More aliases: \"gremio fbpa\"? not present. \"internacional rs\"? base already has rs... BR-Football has both \"Internacional\" and \"Internacional RS\" — canonical \"internacional rs\" both. \"EC Juventude\"/\"Juventude\" → \"juventude rs\". \"EC Vitoria\"/\"Vitoria EC\"/\"Vitoria\" → \"vitoria ba\". \"Vitoria da Conquista\" → distinct (ES? no — BA! Vitória da Conquista is Bahia). Hmm wait BR-Football 'Vitoria da Conquista' — Bahia state, distinct club. Cup has 'Vitória da Conquista - BA'. OK.\n     \"Atletico Goianiense\" → \"atletico go\". \"Atletico Acreano\" → \"atletico ac\". \"Atletico Alagoinhas\" → \"atletico ba\"? Hmm — Atlético de Alagoinhas (BA). Cup: 'Atlético - BA' — same club! Alias \"atletico alagoinhas\"→\"atletico\" with BA. \"Atletico Cearense\"/\"FC Atlético Cearense\" → \"atletico cearense ce\" — Cup has 'Atlético Cearense - CE' — same, keep as \"atletico cearense ce\"? Wait, but then \"atletico\" + CE (Atlético Cearense IS the base...) — no: Atlético Cearense's base is \"atletico cearense\", distinct from \"atletico\". Keep separate. Good.\n     \"Nautico Capibaribe\" → \"nautico pe\". \"Santa Cruz FC\" → \"santa cruz pe\". \"Sport Recife\" → \"sport pe\". \"Vasco Da Gama RJ\" → \"vasco da gama rj\". \"America FC Natal\" → \"america rn\". \"America FC (Minas Gerais)\" → paren removed → \"america fc\" → alias \"america fc\"→\"america\" + MG hint. Hmm — \"america fc\" bare appears in BR-Football? 'America FC Natal' full string. After paren removal of \"(Minas Gerais)\": \"america fc\" — need MG. Where's MG from? The paren content \"(Minas Gerais)\"! I could map state names → abbreviations from parenthetical: \"minas gerais\"→MG. Nice touch — handle \"América FC (Minas Gerais)\".\n     \"Portuguesa Desportos\" → \"portuguesa sp\". \"Guarani de Juazeiro\" → \"guarani ce\"? Cup has \"Guarani de Juazeiro - CE\" and \"Guarany - CE\" and \"Guarany de Sobral - CE\" — Guarani de Juazeiro vs Guarany de Sobral are different clubs. BR-Football \"Guarani de Juazeiro\" (no state) — with alias → \"guarani ce\". Hmm but is BR-Football's \"Guarani de Juazeiro\" the same as Cup's \"Guarani de Juazeiro - CE\"? Presumably yes.\n     \"Macae Esporte FC\"/\"Macae Esporte RJ\" → \"macae rj\". \"Esportivo Bento Goncalves\"? Hmm — \"Esportivo - RS\" in Cup vs \"Esportivo Bento Goncalves\" in BR-Football — same club (Esportivo de Bento Gonçalves RS). Alias \"esportivo bento goncalves\"→\"esportivo\" RS. \"Gremio Anapolis\" → \"gremio anapolis\" GO — distinct. \"Gremio Novorizontino\" → Grêmio Novorizontino SP vs Cup \"Novorizontino - SP\" — alias \"gremio novorizontino\"→\"novorizontino\" SP. \"Grêmio Barueri - SP\" vs novo \"Barueri\" — alias \"gremio barueri\"→\"barueri\" SP. \"Atletico Acreano\" → \"atletico ac\" vs Cup \"Atlético - AC\" — alias \"atletico acreano\"→\"atletico\"+AC. \n     \"Pstc - PR\" → \"pstc pr\" (Panambi? no — PSTC = Paraná). fine as-is.\n     \"Xv de Piracicaba - SP\" → \"xv de piracicaba sp\". \"Inter de Limeira - SP\" → \"inter de limeira sp\" (distinct from Internacional). \"A.b.c.\" → \"abc rn\". \"Asa - AL\"/\"A.s.a.\" → \"asa al\". \"Crb - AL\"/\"C.r.b.\" → \"crb al\". \"Csa - AL\"/\"C.s.a.\" → \"csa al\". \"S.francisco - PA\" → \"sfrancisco pa\"? Hmm \"S.francisco\" dots-removed → \"sfrancisco\". Keep.\n     \"Sampaio Correa\"/\"Sampaio Corrêa\" → accents stripped → same \"sampaio correa ma\". \n     \"Rio Branco - Vn - ES\" → state strip gets \"es\" trailing; base \"rio branco vn\" → alias \"rio branco vn\"→\"rio branco\" ES.\n     \"Boavista Sport Club (antigo Esporte Clube Barreira) - RJ\" → paren removed → \"boavista sport club rj\" → alias → \"boavista rj\".\n     \"Juventude - MA\" vs \"EC Juventude\"(RS) vs \"Juventude - RS\" — \"juventude ma\" is a small club; bare \"Juventude\" in BR-Football with state hint RS. OK.\n     \"Athletico\" (Libertadores, no state) → alias \"athletico\" → \"atletico pr\". Wait — careful: Libertadores file — which Brazilian teams appear without suffix? Flamengo, Athletico, Gremio? Let me check Libertadores Brazilian team names. Earlier: \"Nacional (URU)\", \"Barcelona-EQU\" — foreign teams have country suffixes in parens/abbrevs. Need special handling: \"Barcelona-EQU\" — hmm, \"-EQU\" is 3 letters. In Libertadores, Brazilian clubs: let me check how they appear (\"Flamengo\" vs \"Flamengo-RJ\"?). The NA final row had 'Flamengo' and 'Athletico' bare. Let me enumerate Libertadores team names.\n\nLet me also check the novo file teams fully (43) and the date parsing edge cases (some \"Data\" values might be empty or odd formats).\n\nAlso FIFA \"Club\" values include NaN? There's a leading unnamed index column. 18,207 players. Let me check how many have empty Club.\n\nLet me run these checks.",