ly rounds with small clubs)? 111 matches in 2021 (real: ~92)?? Hmm 111 > 92. Whatever — completeness aside, br_football 2021 includes the final while the cup file doesn't. Let me compare counts per season for both sources. Real Copa do Brasil: 2014: 87 matches? Actually from 2015: 91? The counts (108 in cup file 2021 vs 111 in br_football) both exceed the true 92... They might both include preliminaries I'm not counting. Not important.\n\nDecision: copa_do_brasil default source priority: ['brazilian_cup', 'br_football'] per season EXCEPT where the cup-file season is incomplete — how do I detect \"incomplete\"? NA-goal matches! If a source has matches with missing goals (NA), those are unrecorded. Rule: when choosing default source for (comp, season), prefer a source with NO missing-goals matches; among those, use priority order. So 2021 → br_football (cup file has NA rows); 2012-2013 → brazilian_cup only; 2014-2020 → brazilian_cup (complete, has round numbers) — but wait, do the two sources disagree on matches for 2014-2020? Both have full data presumably (with some name/round differences). For \"find finals\" I use max-round within the cup file or last-date in br_football. Hmm — for finals detection I need a robust approach: final = matches in the last \"round\" of that season (by round number if available, else by date: the latest distinct date... risky).\n\nAlternative simpler + robust: for cup competitions, \"final\" = the last TWO matches (by date) if they're between the same pair of teams (two-legged), else the last ONE match. Implement `finals(comp, season)`: take matches sorted by date; look at last two; if same team pair (either order) → two-legged final; else last match alone is the final (single match). For Libertadores use stage=='final' (explicit). For Copa do Brasil use the date heuristic on default source. For 2023: Flamengo 0-1 São Paulo / São Paulo 1-1 Flamengo → aggregate São Paulo 2-1 → champion São Paulo (2023 Copa do Brasil winner — correct! São Paulo beat Flamengo).\n\nAlso \"Find all Copa do Brasil finals\" (spec example) — list finals across seasons.\n\n5. **Round 8 in cup file = final** for 2013-2015, 2017-2020. 2012: max 6 (2012 final = Corinthians vs Palmeiras? hmm the file has rounds 1-6 with 112 matches; 2012 Copa do Brasil... the final was Corinthians vs Palmeiras? No — 2012: Palmeiras beat Coritiba in the final). Anyway the date-based finals heuristic handles all seasons uniformly. I don't need round numbers for finals.\n\n6. For standings of leagues — I use default source per season; both brasileirao and novo and br_football are complete for overlapping seasons. Priority: brasileirao (2012-2022), br_football (2023, and 2014-2023 as backup), novo (2003-2011). BUT — hmm, one consideration: for Brasileirão 2020: brasileirao file has how many matches? COVID year — still 380. Fine.\n\nWait, actually — should I prefer br_football over brasileirao for 2014-2022 Serie A since it has richer stats (corners/shots)? The dedicated Brasileirão file has explicit round numbers (\"Round 22\" in example answers) — the spec's example format shows \"(Brasileirão Round 22)\". br_football has NO round info. So brasileirao file preferred for Serie A. Round numbers matter for display. novo also has Rodada. OK:\n- serie_a priority: brasileirao > novo > br_football (with NA-check). Coverage: 2003-2011 novo, 2012-2022 brasileirao, 2023 br_football. But wait — novo 2003-2005: 552/552/462 matches (24-team era) — fine, standings computable.\n- serie_b/serie_c: br_football only.\n- libertadores: libertadores only.\n- copa_do_brasil: brazilian_cup > br_football, with NA-season fallback → 2021 uses br_football.\n\nHmm — but hold on: for copa_do_brasil 2014-2020, does brazilian_cup have ALL the matches that br_football has? E.g. 2019: cup file has 120 matches. br_football 2019 Copa do Brasil: let me count. If br_football has matches missing from the cup file, then \"all matches of team X in Copa do Brasil 2019\" might miss some. The cup file should be complete for those seasons (it's the dedicated file). Fine.\n\nActually wait — one more thing to verify: br_football \"Fortaleza FC\" — fold (\"fortaleza fc\", None) → (\"fortaleza\", \"ce\")? It appears in Copa do Brasil rows. \"Fortaleza\" bare in BR-Football Serie A → hint ce. Add fold for \"fortaleza fc\"→fortaleza+ce. Similarly \"Atletico Mineiro\" bare (br_football) → fold mg. Seen above. OK.\n\n7. **Fixture overlap between brazilian_cup & br_football for 2014-2020** — after default-source selection per (comp, season), each fixture appears once. But cross-checking same fixture in both files with slightly different dates? Doesn't matter — default source per season prevents double counting entirely.\n\nBUT what about within-source duplicates: e.g., br_football Serie A 2020 vs 2020 (covid rescheduling) — no. And cup file might have duplicate fixtures? The dedup key (season, comp, home, away, date) will guard.\n\nHmm, wait — there's one more cross-source subtlety: **Libertadores + Serie A both in 2012-2022 — no overlap** (different comps). Fine.\n\nAnd: the same fixture could appear in BOTH brazilian_cup and br_football Copa do Brasil for 2014-2020 — handled by source priority. \n\n8. **2021 Libertadores final missing entirely** (Palmeiras 2-1 Flamengo, single match Nov 27 2021). Semifinals exist? Let me check 2021 semifinal rows — if the file just lacks the final, champion(2021) → \"no final in dataset\". Data-quality note. Similarly 2022 final placeholder NA. I'll document these.\n\nNow — plan the module layout concretely and write the code. Let me also verify the mcp Python SDK 2.1.1 API (FastMCP import path, tool decoration, ClientSession usage) with a quick smoke test before writing everything.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05c014fd7001ETbRHsruHyTGXd","sessionID":"ses_fa40f0460ffeYpKGnJPSwDwXVy","messageID":"msg_05bfff47a001tQrXbU67mA1V0n"}}
