\" bare, \"Cuiaba\" bare, \"Ponte Preta\"? Let me dump ALL BR-Football teams to be thorough when writing hints. I'll do that during implementation and verify with a coverage test: every raw team name maps to an id; report any team whose id is stateless and appears only once (data-quality view).\n\nFIFA clubs: \"Botafogo\"→rj hint, \"Santos\"→sp, \"Grêmio\"→rs, \"Internacional\"→rs, \"Bahia\"→ba, \"Vitória\"→ba, \"Cruzeiro\"→mg, \"Fluminense\"→rj, \"Paraná\"→pr, \"Chapecoense\"→sc, folds for others. Foreign FIFA clubs: register as-is (stateless foreign). Note FIFA \"Paraná\" — hint pr ✓.\n\nFIFA \"América FC (Minas Gerais)\" → paren-state → america mg ✓. \"Ceará Sporting Club\" → fold ceara ce ✓. \"Sport Club do Recife\" → fold sport pe ✓. \"Atlético Mineiro\" → fold atletico mg ✓. \"Atlético Paranaense\" → fold atletico pr ✓.\n\nNow the REGISTRY and resolution:\n\n```python\nclass TeamRegistry:\n    entities: dict[str, TeamEntity]\n    def register_raw(raw_name, source) -> team_id\n    def resolve(query) -> TeamResolution\n```\n\nresolve(query):\n1. parse query → (base, state, country)\n2. fold aliases\n3. candidate ids:\n   - if country: id = f\"{base} {country}\" → exact\n   - if state: id = f\"{base} {state}\" → exact; also check \"base alone\"?\n   - no state: \n     a. id == base exists (stateless entity, e.g. \"boca juniors\") → exact\n     b. STATE_HINTS[base] → id\n     c. all entities with team_id == base or team_id startswith base+\" \" → candidates\n     d. if 1 → it; if >1 → ambiguous (list), if 0 → fuzzy substring\n4. fuzzy: substring/token-overlap matches on bases; return best or candidates.\n\nAlso handle query with hyphen state like \"Atletico-MG\" — parse handles. Query \"São Paulo\" → \"sao paulo\" + hint sp. Query \"Fla\"? skip nicknames... Actually let me add a few popular NICKNAMES for usability: \"fla\"→flamengo? The spec doesn't require it. Skip.\n\nAlso note — the query \"Sport\" (base) → hint pe → \"sport pe\" ✓. Query \"Sport Recife\" → fold → sport pe ✓. Query \"Athletico\" → fold atletico pr — wait ALIASES ('athletico', None) → ('atletico','pr') — yes resolves directly to atletico pr. Good — because bare \"athletico\" only ever means Athletico-PR.\n\nQuery \"Atletico\" → base atletico, no state, no hint → candidates: atletico mg, atletico pr, atletico go, atletico ba, atletico ac, atletico cearense ce? (base \"atletico cearense\" — startswith \"atletico \" → included). Ambiguous → disambiguation message. \n\nQuery \"América\" → candidates america mg, america rn (+ foreign america de cali? id \"america de cali\" startswith \"america\"?? — my candidates rule: team_id == base or startswith(base + \" \") — \"america de cali\" starts with \"america \" → included — hmm, that's actually reasonable: list them all. Maybe limit to Brazilian? For disambiguation message it's fine to include foreign with a note.\n\nHmm, wait — subtle: query \"Santos\" with candidates: \"santos sp\" (hint applied → resolves immediately). But what if user wants Santos-AP? They type \"Santos-AP\" → state ap → exact \"santos ap\" ✓. \n\nNow — for the response when hint-resolved with existing alternatives, include \"Did you mean\" note. \n\n**Competition normalization**:\n```python\nCOMPETITIONS = {\n 'serie_a': CompetitionInfo(id='serie_a', display='Brasileirão Série A', aliases=['brasileirao','serie a','brasileirão série a','campeonato brasileiro','serie a (brasileirao)',...], type='league'),\n 'serie_b': ... 'Série B', league\n 'serie_c': ... 'Série C', league\n 'copa_do_brasil': cup\n 'libertadores': cup\n}\n```\nBR-Football tournament names map: 'Serie A'→serie_a, 'Serie B'→serie_b, 'Serie C'→serie_c, 'Copa do Brasil'→copa_do_brasil.\n\nQuery resolution: normalize accents/lower, match against aliases.\n\n**Date parsing**: \n- \"2012-05-19 18:30:00\" → date\n- \"2023-09-24\" → date\n- \"29/03/2003\" → date\n- \"NA\" → None\nAlso accept query inputs \"2023-09-24\", \"24/09/2023\", \"2023\". date_from/date_to inclusive.\n\n**Now the loader**:\n\n```python\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass Match:\n    match_id: str           # source-specific unique\n    date: date | None\n    season: str\n    competition: str        # comp id\n    source: str\n    home_team: str          # team_id\n    away_team: str\n    home_goals: int\n    away_goals: int\n    round_label: str | None  # \"Round 22\", \"Final\", \"Group Stage\" etc.\n    stadium: str | None\n    stats: MatchStats | None  # corners/shots/attacks (br_football)\n    raw_home: str\n    raw_away: str\n```\n\nLoader steps:\n1. Load each CSV → list[Match], skipping rows with unparseable goals or missing teams. Count skipped per source (data-quality).\n2. Build team registry from match files + FIFA clubs.\n3. Compute default source per (competition, season): sources present ordered by priority; prefer source with zero missing matches — hmm, \"missing\" detection: I skip NA rows at load. Then how do I know the cup file 2021 is incomplete? The NA rows are SKIPPED — I need to count \"rows with missing goals\" per (source, comp, season) BEFORE skipping. So: `data_quality: dict[(source), dict]` with counts: rows_total, rows_skipped. And per (source, comp, season): skipped count. Default source selection: among sources with skipped==0 for that (comp, season), pick highest priority; if all have skipped>0, pick priority order anyway.\n   - copa_do_brasil 2021: brazilian_cup skipped=16 → br_football chosen ✓.\n   - libertadores 2022: skipped=1 (NA row) → only source → chosen anyway.\n4. Build match indexes: by team, by (comp, season), etc. Provide `iter_matches(competition=None, season=None, team=None, opponent=None, date_range, stage, source=None, dedupe=True)`.\n\nDedup logic: if source is None → for each (comp, season) use default source. If source given → only that source. Within result, dedupe identical (date, home, away) — for safety.\n\nHmm wait — there's a wrinkle: if user asks matches for team=\"Flamengo\", competition=None, season=None: we iterate all comps/seasons with default sources → no dupes across sources ✓.\n\n**Source display names**: 'Brasileirão Matches (Kaggle ricardomattos05)', etc. — keep ids short: 'brasileirao', 'copa_do_brasil_file'... hmm — name clash with comp id. Use source ids: 'kaggle_brasileirao', 'kaggle_copa_do_brasil', 'kaggle_libertadores', 'br_football', 'kaggle_historico' (novo), 'fifa'. Display: \"Brasileirao_Matches.csv\", etc.\n\n**Priorities**:\n- serie_a: ['kaggle_brasileirao', 'kaggle_historico', 'br_football'] — hmm wait, order matters: 2012-2019 overlap brasileirao & historico (both fine) → brasileirao first. 2014-2023 overlap with br_football → brasileirao first. 2023 only br_football. 2003-2011 only historico. But historico also has 2003-2005 odd formats (552 matches) — standings computed over whatever matches exist ✓.\n- copa_do_brasil: ['kaggle_copa_do_brasil', 'br_football'] — cup file preferred for round labels; 2021 falls back to br_football due to NA-skips; 2022-2023 only br_football.\n- libertadores: ['kaggle_libertadores']\n- serie_b/serie_c: ['br_football']\n\n**Standings computation**: from matches of (comp, season) default source. Team stats: per team: played, w, d, l, gf, ga, pts. Sort pts desc, then w, then gd, then gf. Return list[StandingRow]. Champion = row 1. Relegated = last 4 (flag). For 2003-2005 (24 teams), last 4 still flagged (caveat in README).\n\n**Head-to-head**: matches where {home,away} == {a,b}; record from a's perspective (wins/draws/losses), goals. Include per-comp breakdown optionally.\n\n**team_stats**: venue filter: home/away/all. Competition/season filters. Output: matches, w/d/l, gf, ga, win rate.\n\n**Player queries**: \n```python\n@dataclass\nclass Player:\n    id, name, age, nationality, overall, potential, club, position, jersey, height, weight, value, wage, skills: dict[str,int|None], preferred_foot\n```\nsearch_players(name=None, nationality=None, club=None, position=None, min_overall=None, max_age=None, limit): returns list sorted by overall desc then name. Club matching: resolve club via team registry (fifa club names registered!) — e.g. club=\"Flamengo\" → flamengo rj → fifa raw clubs {\"Flamengo\"}? FIFA has no Flamengo → 0 results + helpful note? Hmm — \"Which players play for Flamengo?\" is a spec sample question! FIFA 19 has no Flamengo club (not in the licensed list). Let me double check — maybe Flamengo IS there. Earlier grep of clubs for 'flamengo' found nothing. So the honest answer: \"No players found at Flamengo in the FIFA dataset (the dataset covers clubs licensed in FIFA 19; Brazilian coverage is limited to ...). Brazilian players at Brazilian clubs in the dataset: ...\" — the tool can add that context. That's honest data handling. The sample answer format shows Flamengo with 8 players — but that's aspirational (\"...\"). Our data says otherwise; being data-truthful is correct. I'll make `team_players`/club filter ALSO fall back to substring match on club raw names, so e.g. \"Santos\" matches \"Santos\" AND \"Santos Laguna\"? No — that'd be wrong (different club). Resolve via registry → exact fifa club name mapping. Good.\n\nAlso nationality: \"Brazil\" — normalize accent/case (\"Brasil\" → \"Brazil\"? FIFA uses English names: \"Brazil\". Accept both: fold accents/case; map \"brasil\"→\"brazil\" etc.? Simple casefold + accent strip match on raw nationality is fine; maybe add a couple common aliases (Brasil→Brazil, Argentina...). I'll do casefold+accent-strip exact, else substring.\n\n**position group helper**: FWD/DEF/MID/GK as described.\n\n**find_player(name)**: substring accent-insensitive; return all matches with detail block (name, age, nationality, ratings, club, position, jersey, key attributes).\n\n**top_players(...)**: sort by overall or a chosen attribute (e.g. \"Finishing\"). Attributes dict from skills.\n\n**Derbies** table:\n```python\nDERBIES = [\n  ('flamengo rj','fluminense rj','Fla-Flu'),\n  ('flamengo rj','vasco da gama rj','Clássico dos Milhões'),\n  ('gremio rs','internacional rs','Grenallo'),\n  ('palmeiras sp','corinthians sp','Derby Paulista'),\n  ('corinthians sp','sao paulo sp','Majestoso'),\n  ('palmeiras sp','sao paulo sp','Choque-Rei'),\n  ('cruzeiro mg','atletico mg','Clássico Mineiro'),\n  ('bahia ba','vitoria ba','Ba-Vi'),\n]\n```\n\n**biggest_wins**: margin desc, then total goals desc; filter comp/season/team; limit; include competition label in output line.\n\n**average_goals**: per comp/season/all: avg goals/match, home-win %, draw %, away-win %, avg home goals, avg away goals. Also team filter → team's matches only.\n\n**compare_teams**: two teams side-by-side (overall record across all comps in dataset, or filtered), plus H2H summary line.\n\n**list_competitions**: for each comp: display, type, seasons (from-to), match count, sources with counts.\n\n**find_team**: resolve + entity info: display, state, variants, comps played w/ seasons, total matches, W/D/L overall? Keep: comps & seasons & match count + variants. \n\n**last_match(team, opponent=None)**: latest date match (dedup default), return formatted with date/score/comp.\n\n**champion(competition, season)**: league → standings[0]; cup → finals analysis (aggregate). Output format like spec.\n\n**finals(competition, season=None)**: list finals (libertadores stage='final'; copa_do_brasil via date heuristic) with aggregate results. Also handles \"Find all Copa do Brasil finals\".\n\nWait — for copa_do_brasil finals detection via \"last two matches same pair\" heuristic — need care with 2012 (cup file max round 6; last two matches by date — 2012 final was Palmeiras vs Coritiba two-legged in Nov/Dec 2012). Let me trust the heuristic. But 2021 br_football: last two by date: Dec 12 Atletico Mineiro 4-0 Athletico; Dec 16 Athletico 1-2 Atletico Mineiro — same pair ✓ aggregate AM 6-1 → champion Atletico Mineiro. ✓. 2022: Corinthians 0-0 Flamengo (Oct 13); Flamengo 1-1 Corinthians (Oct 20) — aggregate 1-1 → penalties → \"winner not determinable from data\" (real: Flamengo on pens). ✓ honest. 2023: São Paulo 2-1 agg ✓.\n\nEdge: what if last two matches by date are NOT same pair (e.g. simultaneously played last round)? Then single final = last match only? Risky. Better heuristic: group matches by round_label if available (cup file has round numbers; final = max round); for br_football (no round), use the date-pair heuristic. For cup file 2012: max round 6 → final. 2021 cup file skipped (default source br_football). Let me verify cup file finals per season: round max per season: 2012:6, 2013:8, 2014:8, 2015:8, 2016:7, 2017:8, 2018:8, 2019:8, 2020:8. Real Copa do Brasil finals: 2012: Palmeiras-Coritiba (round... whatever), 2016: Grêmio-Cruzeiro? The file's round numbering for 2016 max 7 — fine, final = max round matches. Are finals always in max round? For a knockout cup yes — final is the last round. ✓. For br_football Copa do Brasil (2021-2023 by default): date-pair heuristic ✓.\n\nSo `finals()` impl:\n- libertadores: matches with stage=='final' (round_label 'Final'), grouped by season.\n- copa_do_brasil: per (season, default source): if matches have round labels (cup file → round numbers): final = matches with max round. Else (br_football): sort by date; last two; if same pair → both; else last one.\n- Champion: aggregate goals; if tie → None (penalties note). For single match → winner or draw→None.\n\nFor Libertadores two-legged finals: aggregate across both. 2013: Olimpia 2-0 Atletico-MG; Atletico-MG 2-0 Olimpia → agg 2-2 → penalties (Atletico-MG won — real). Data says tie → \"decided on penalties; winner not in dataset\". Hmm — the 2013 final: Atlético-MG won on penalties. Our tool reports the aggregate and notes penalties. For the answer \"Who won the 2019 Libertadores?\" → single match Flamengo 2-1 River → Flamengo ✓ (real: Flamengo won 2019). \n\n**\"Show the 2018 Copa Libertadores bracket\"**: tool `knockout_bracket(competition, season)` — for libertadores: list stage-grouped knockout matches (round of 16, QF, SF, final) with aggregate per pairing? Aggregates for earlier rounds too — nice but complex. Simpler: list matches grouped by stage with scores; plus finals aggregate + champion. I'll implement `knockout(competition, season)` returning matches grouped by stage/round for knockout comps. Good enough for \"bracket\".\n\n**average_goals** and **biggest_wins** — straightforward.\n\n**Statistical extras**: \"Which team has the best away record?\" → `best_records(venue='away', competition=None, season=None, limit)` — rank teams by win rate (min matches threshold, e.g. ≥ 20? or ≥10). I'll add `team_rankings(metric=..., ...)`? Keep focused: `best_home_record`/`best_away_record` via one tool `best_records(venue, competition, season, min_matches, metric)` — metric: 'win_rate' | 'goals' etc. Hmm — keep it simple: rank by win rate. Also \"Which team scored the most goals in Serie A 2023?\" → standings covers goals (GF column) — standings answer includes top scorers by goals. I'll note in standings output \"Most goals: X (Y)\". So no separate tool needed... but a dedicated `top_teams_by(...)` might be nice. Skip — standings covers it.\n\nTool list final (name → purpose):\n1. `list_competitions()` \n2. `find_team(name)` \n3. `list_teams(competition, season)` \n4. `search_matches(team, opponent, competition, season, stage, date_from, date_to, limit, source)` \n5. `head_to_head(team_a, team_b, competition, season)` \n6. `team_stats(team, competition, season, venue)` \n7. `compare_teams(team_a, team_b, competition, season)` \n8. `search_players(name, nationality, club, position, min_overall, max_age, limit)` \n9. `top_players(nationality, club, position, limit, attribute)` \n10. `find_player(name)` \n11. `team_players(team, limit)` \n12. `standings(competition, season)` \n13. `champion(competition, season)` \n14. `finals(competition, season)` \n15. `knockout_bracket(competition, season)` \n16. `biggest_wins(competition, season, team, limit)` \n17. `competition_stats(competition, season)` — avg goals, home/away/draw rates \n18. `derbies(season, competition, limit)` \n19. `team_competitions(team)` \n20. `last_match(team, opponent)` \n\n20 tools — nice. Each returns formatted text. \n\nNow **GWT/BDD test harness**. I'll write `soccer_mcp/bdd.py`:\n\n```python\nclass StepFailure(AssertionError): ...\n\nclass Scenario:\n    def __init__(self, feature, name):\n        self.feature = feature; self.name = name; self.context = {}\n        self._steps = []\n    def given(self, description, **facts):   # register facts into context\n        record and update context; return self\n    def when(self, description, **actions):  # actions are callables taking context → value stored under key\n    def then(self, description, **expectations):  # callables taking context; raise AssertionError on failure\n    def run(self):  # execute steps in order, pretty-print trace on failure\n```\n\nHmm — but pytest collects test functions; the Scenario.run() should be called inside test functions. Failure reporting: catch AssertionError, re-raise with scenario/step context via `pytest.fail` message? I'll raise AssertionError with a formatted message including feature/scenario/step. \n\nSimplest ergonomic API:\n\n```python\ndef test_find_matches_between_two_teams():\n    (\n        Scenario(\"Match Queries\", \"Find matches between two teams\")\n        .given(\"the match data is loaded\", dataset=dataset)\n        .when(\"I search for matches between 'Flamengo' and 'Fluminense'\",\n              result=lambda ctx: search_matches(ctx[\"dataset\"], team=\"Flamengo\", opponent=\"Fluminense\"))\n        .then(\"I should receive a list of matches\",\n              lambda ctx: assert_true(len(ctx[\"result\"]) > 0))\n        .then(\"each match should have date, scores, and competition\",\n              lambda ctx: all(m.date and m.competition for m in ctx[\"result\"]))\n        .run()\n    )\n```\n\nwhere `given(..., **facts)` stores each fact in ctx; `when(..., **actions)` each callable(ctx) → stored; `then(desc, fn)` fn(ctx) returns None or raises. The `.run()` executes with step tracking. Also `then_expect(**{key: fn})`? Keep `then(desc, assertion)` where assertion is a callable; provide helpers `equals`, `greater_than`, `contains`, `is_true`, `is_none` returning assertion closures with informative messages. I'll implement small helpers.\n\nThis gives genuinely BDD-GWT-structured pytest without extra deps. \n\n**MCP server end-to-end test**: in-memory client session over the real tool registration (no subprocess needed — avoids venv path issues and is faster; but subprocess stdio test is closer to real usage... in-memory is fine and robust; I'll ALSO do a stdio subprocess smoke test? The subprocess approach: `StdioServerParameters(command=sys.executable, args=[\"server.py\"])` with cwd=repo root — the server prints nothing to stdout except protocol (data loading must not print!). I'll ensure no prints in server startup. Let me include the subprocess test but guard with a timeout. Actually — data loading ~1-2s, stdio handshake — fine. I'll write both: in-memory tool-call tests (most), plus one stdio subprocess smoke test (list tools + one call). Hmm — subprocess test could be flaky in CI... I'll make it robust with generous timeout (60s) and skip-if-no-module guard. Let me do it — real end-to-end verification is valuable. Actually, let me reconsider: the in-memory test exercises the same MCPServer + tools; the stdio layer is the SDK's. The subprocess adds venv/paths risk (e.g., if `python` in PATH differs). Using sys.executable avoids that. OK, include it.\n\n**Performance test**: mark with `@pytest.mark.perf` — simple lookups < 2s, aggregate < 5s (measured on cached dataset). These are trivially fast in-memory; include as sanity.\n\n**Sample-questions test**: parametrized 20+ questions → tool calls → non-trivial output.\n\n**requirements.txt**: mcp>=2.1,<3; pytest; pytest-cov (already present). Also `pandas`? NO — I'll use stdlib csv (no pandas dep — faster startup, fewer deps). Good.\n\n**README**: document implementation, architecture, tools, how to run server (stdio config example for Claude Desktop / opencode), how to run tests, data-quality notes (skipped NA rows, 2021 Libertadores final missing, etc.), sample questions list (20+), licenses/attribution.\n\nAlso the guide mentions maintaining \"a detailed context block comment at the start of every code file\" — from prompts.txt sample (ignored) — but I'll still write a rich module docstring for each file; costs little, aids the benchmark.\n\n**Directory layout** — code in `soccer_mcp/` package, `server.py` at root, tests in `tests/`.\n\nOne more thing — check for other BR-Football bare team names I haven't covered. Let me dump the full team lists to files and eyeball them while writing hints. Also check FIFA \"Club\" NaN handling (241 empty), Position '' (60).\n\nAlso the odd goals in Libertadores: 2 rows (the NA one and one more?). \"odd goals rows: 2\" — includes NA row; the other? Let me check quickly. Also cup: 16 NA rows (2021 round 4) ✓ already seen.\n\nLet me check Libertadores odd rows + BR-Football full team list.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05c07eeb4001FeFFFi7tfnVyTY","sessionID":"ses_fa40f0460ffeYpKGnJPSwDwXVy","messageID":"msg_05c02f9c7001jRwvJfkF1x61RW"}}
