quirements.txt, pytest.ini, update README.md","status":"pending","priority":"medium"},{"content":"Run full test suite, fix issues, verify MCP server boots","status":"pending","priority":"high"}],"truncated":false},"title":"9 todos","time":{"start":1788250798327,"end":1788250798328}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"- 'Ypiranga' bare: appears in Serie C 2017 (with Tombense MG, etc.) AND Copa do Brasil 2019 vs Fluminense. Serie C Ypiranga = Ypiranga-RS? Ypiranga-AP played Serie C too... In 2017 Serie C, Ypiranga-RS was in it (Ypiranga de Erechim). And Copa do Brasil 2019 \"Ypiranga\" vs Fluminense — Ypiranga-AP played Fluminense in 2019 CdB? Hmm, actually Ypiranga-AP did play Copa do Brasil 2019 (they faced Fluminense? I believe Ypiranga-AP vs Fluminense happened in 2019 CdB first round? not sure). This is genuinely ambiguous — the bare 'Ypiranga' rows probably mix both clubs. Since both 'Ypiranga AP' and 'Ypiranga RS' also appear explicitly, the safest is: bare 'Ypiranga' stays its own stateless entity 'ypiranga' (data-quality imperfection, documented). OK.\n- 'CA Parana': 1 match, Serie B 2015 vs Bragantino. In 2015 Serie B... CA Paranaense?? Athletico-PR was in Serie A 2015. Serie B 2015 had... \"CA Parana\" — I think this is actually a data error or maybe it's \"CA Paranaense\" — but Athletico was Serie A 2015. Wait — Serie B 2015: who from Paraná? Coritiba was Serie A... Operário? no. Londrina? no (Serie B 2015 had Londrina? maybe). Hmm — \"CA Parana\" 1 match only vs Bragantino. Whatever it is, keep as own entity. Fine.\n- 'Monsoon FC' — 1 match Serie B 2022 (weird name, data quirk) — keep as-is.\n- 'Real FC' — 1 match CdB 2021 vs America RN — Real FC (Ariquemes?) — keep as-is.\n- 'Sao Jose - POA' → fold to ('sao jose','rs') ✓ as planned.\n\nEnough. Now write the implementation. I'll create a todo list to track, then write files.\n\nDesign recap for the package:\n\n**soccer_mcp/model.py** — dataclasses: Match, MatchStats, Player, TeamEntity, StandingRow, FinalResult, HeadToHead, CompetitionInfo, TeamRecord (stats bundle), TeamResolution.\n\n**soccer_mcp/normalize.py**:\n- VALID_UFS (27)\n- STATE_NAMES for parens\n- COUNTRY_CODES\n- strip_accents, _clean_base, join_single_letter_runs\n- ALIASES: {(base, state_or_None): (base, state)}\n- FAMOUS_STATE_HINTS: {base: state} (only needed where auto-derivation is ambiguous)\n- parse_team_name(raw) -> ParsedName(base, state, country, paren_state)\n- canonical_team_id(parsed) -> str\n- normalize_competition(query) -> comp_id\n- parse_date_any(s) -> date|None\n- position_group(pos)\n- text_key(s) (accent-stripped lowercase for name/nationality search)\n\n**soccer_mcp/data_loader.py**:\n- CSV_SPECS definitions\n- load_players, load each match file\n- TeamRegistry class w/ two-pass registration + auto state derivation\n- SoccerData: matches (list), players, registry, source_priority resolution, data_quality report\n- iter_matches(...) with dedup via default-source selection\n- dataset cache: get_dataset(data_dir=None) — loads once; data_dir default = repo_root/data/kaggle (relative to package parent).\n- COMPETITION definitions + SOURCE_PRIORITY.\n\n**soccer_mcp/queries.py** — functions returning dict/dataclass results:\n- resolve_team(dataset, name) -> TeamResolution\n- search_matches(...) -> list[Match]\n- head_to_head(...) -> dict\n- team_stats(...) -> TeamRecord + sub-records\n- team_competitions(...)\n- search_players(...) / top_players(...) / find_players_by_name(...)\n- standings(...) -> list[StandingRow]\n- champion(...) / finals(...) / knockout(...)\n- biggest_wins(...), competition_stats(...), derbies(...), last_match(...)\n- list_competitions(), list_teams(comp, season)\n\n**soccer_mcp/formatting.py** — render each result type to text per spec formats.\n\n**soccer_mcp/tools.py** — tool functions calling queries+formatting with dataset singleton. These get registered on MCPServer in server.py. Docstrings = tool descriptions.\n\n**server.py** — create MCPServer, register tools, `main()` → run stdio.\n\n**soccer_mcp/bdd.py** — GWT harness.\n\n**tests/** — conftest (dataset fixture, scenario import), test files as planned + test_mcp_server.py + test_sample_questions.py + test_performance.py.\n\nAlso `requirements.txt`, `pytest.ini`, README update.\n\nImportant: NO print statements in server code (stdio protocol!). Logging → stderr only if needed (skip entirely).\n\nEdge considerations for queries:\n- search_matches default limit=20, but return total count; format like spec: \"... (15 more matches in dataset)\".\n- All tools must handle None/empty results gracefully with helpful messages.\n\nFor `standings` with 2003-2005 seasons (24 teams): standings computed from matches — fine. relegated = bottom 4 flagged (note caveat).\n\nTeamRecord computation: iterate matches where team is home or away.\n\nHead-to-head: matches between a & b; compute wins for a, wins for b, draws; goals; list recent.\n\nChampion for league: standings[0]. For cup: finals aggregate.\n\nLet me also design the finals/champion logic carefully:\n\n```python\ndef finals(dataset, competition, season=None) -> list[FinalResult]\n# FinalResult: season, matches: list[Match], home_agg, away_agg, team_a, team_b, winner, note\n```\nFor libertadores: filter stage label 'Final' (round_label). Group by season.\nFor copa_do_brasil: per (season): matches = default-source matches; if any have round_label with numeric round → final matches = max round. Else sort by date; take last 2 if same pair else last 1.\nWinner: aggregate over legs (leg order = date order); if aggregate equal → winner=None, note \"level on aggregate — decided on penalties (not recorded in dataset)\".\n\nFor single match: winner = match winner or None if draw.\n\n`champion(comp, season)`:\n- league: standings[0] with \"(champion)\".\n- cup: finals → winner or note.\n\n`knockout_bracket(comp, season)`: for libertadores: group by stage: 'round of 16', 'quarterfinals', 'semifinals', 'final' with each tie (pair) aggregated. For copa_do_brasil: group by round number (or date clusters?). For cup file rounds are numeric: label them R1..R8? Real round names unknown — display \"Round N\". For br_football cup (no rounds) — group by nothing; list knockout... just list all matches of season sorted by date with note. Hmm — bracket for Copa do Brasil from br_football source is not groupable without rounds; I'll group Libertadores-style by stage only, and for Copa do Brasil by round label when present, else flat list. Good enough.\n\nFor Libertadores ties aggregation: pair teams (home/away across two matches), aggregate scores. I'll implement `aggregate_ties(matches)`: group by frozenset({a,b}) within a stage, sort each tie's matches by date, compute agg. Two-legged detection: exactly 2 matches for pair. Some pairs might meet only once in group stage — knockout stages are two-legged (except final 2020+ single). For knockout bracket display: only knockout stages.\n\nWait — Libertadores stage values: 'group stage', 'round of 16', 'quarterfinals', 'semifinals', 'final'. ✓.\n\nNow **round_label** per source:\n- brasileirao: f\"Round {round}\"\n- novo: f\"Round {Rodada}\"\n- copa_do_brasil (cup file): f\"Round {round}\" (1-8)\n- libertadores: stage title-cased (\"Group Stage\", \"Round of 16\", \"Quarterfinals\", \"Semifinals\", \"Final\")\n- br_football: None (no round)\n\n`search_matches(stage=...)` filter: match against round_label case-insensitively (\"final\" matches \"Final\").\n\n**stage filter for libertadores group stage**: \"group\" etc.\n\nNow, team display names: pick prettiest variant — prefer the raw variant from the \"primary\" Brazilian sources with accents: e.g. \"Flamengo-RJ\" → display \"Flamengo (RJ)\"? or keep raw \"Flamengo-RJ\"? The spec examples use plain \"Flamengo\". I'll construct display = base title-cased with accents restored?? Restoring accents is hard generically. Simplest: display = the most common raw variant (count across files), but strip the \" - XX\" state suffix and append \" (XX)\" if state: e.g. \"Flamengo (RJ)\", \"Palmeiras (SP)\", \"Atlético-MG\" → hmm raw \"Atlético-MG\" is already nice. Let me do: display = raw variant with most occurrences; if it ends with \"-XX\" or \" - XX\", replace suffix with \" (XX)\". E.g. \"Palmeiras-SP\" → \"Palmeiras (SP)\". \"São Paulo-SP\" → \"São Paulo (SP)\". Keep accents from raw ✓. For foreign: keep raw. For FIFA-only clubs: raw FIFA name.\n\nWait, but \"Athletico Paranaense - PR\" → \"Athletico Paranaense (PR)\" but canonical is \"atletico pr\"... display mismatch is fine (display shows variant). For the canonical Atlético-PR the most common variant is probably \"Atletico-PR\" (brasileirao file) or \"Athletico-PR\". Either fine.\n\nSorting preference for \"most common raw variant\": count by number of matches (not distinct files) — use match occurrences + player counts. Fine.\n\n**Special case**: display for stateless entities: raw as-is.\n\nNow — an important detail for `iter_matches` dedup: the **default source per (comp, season)** is decided by priority + completeness. But when the user gives `source=` explicitly, no dedup needed (single source). Also **within-source** duplicates: dedupe by (date, home, away) always.\n\nLet me also quantify: does brasileirao file have all 380 for each season? 2012-2019: 380 each; 2020-2021: 35? Let me check... 4180 total / 11 seasons ≈ 380. OK fine.\n\nOne more check — novo file \"Vencedor\" column vs goals: I'll compute winner from goals directly (don't trust Vencedor; but could cross-check). Fine.\n\n**Team stats correctness check** for spec example: \"Corinthians home record 2022 Brasileirão: 19 matches, 11W 5D 3L\" — from brasileirao source 2022 ✓ computable.\n\n\"2019 Brasileirão: Flamengo champion with 90 pts\" — real: Flamengo 2019: 90 pts (Santos 74? hmm no — 2019: Flamengo 90, Santos 74? Palmeiras 74? Actually 2019: Flamengo 90, Santos 74? I think: 1. Flamengo 90, 2. Santos 74? no wait — 2019 Serie A: Flamengo 90, Palmeiras 74? Santos 74?? Let me not rely on memory; the computed standings will show whatever the data gives. Real table 2019: 1 Flamengo 90; 2 Santos 74? hmm... I recall 2nd was Santos with 74? or Palmeiras 74? One of them. The spec example shows \"2. Santos - 74 pts, 3. Palmeiras - 74 pts\" — consistent with data presumably. We'll verify in tests loosely (Flamengo 1st, 90 pts — I'll assert Flamengo champion; maybe assert 90 points too — the data should reproduce it since it's complete).\n\nLet me now also confirm the FIFA data has 'Jersey Number' etc. parse — some may be empty. And 'Value'/'Wage' strings like '€110.5M'. Keep raw.\n\nTime to write code. Let me create the todo list and start.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05c0db8f3001zV1GSHJLNzk8Vb","sessionID":"ses_fa40f0460ffeYpKGnJPSwDwXVy","messageID":"msg_05c0c2652001IYWYnUtkk7V4GK"}}
