ss → unique state → inherit; multiple → famous hint; else stateless own entity.\n\nBUT — order problem: registry_states (base → observed states) must be computed from PASS 1 where names parse WITHOUT state inheritance (pure parse + aliases). E.g. \"Botafogo\" bare + \"Botafogo PB\" → base botafogo states {pb, rj(from Botafogo RJ), sp} — wait bare ones contribute no state. States observed: {rj, pb, sp} (from suffixed). Then pass 2: bare \"Botafogo\" → multiple states → hint rj ✓. \"Coritiba\" bare + \"Coritiba - PR\" → states {pr} → inherit pr ✓.\n\nSubtle: FIFA \"Botafogo\" (bare) → pass2 → botafogo rj ✓. FIFA \"Santos\" → {sp, ap} → hint sp ✓. FIFA \"Internacional\" → {rs, sc} → hint rs ✓. FIFA \"Vitória\" → {ba, es} → hint ba ✓. FIFA \"Grêmio\" → {rs} → inherit rs ✓. \n\nNow fix the fold logic bug + remove \"operario\" hint + also \"atletico\": \"mg\" in hints — bare \"atletico\" query → hint mg → Atlético-MG. Hmm — is that right? A user asking \"Atletico\" probably means... could be any. But MG is the most famous \"Atlético\" in Brazil (Galo). Actually Athletico-PR is \"Furacão\", Atlético-MG \"Galo\", Atlético-GO \"Dragão\". For a demo, hinting MG for bare \"Atletico\" is defensible BUT it hides the ambiguity. Better: NO hint for \"atletico\" → ambiguous → disambiguation message. That's more honest. Remove \"atletico\" from hints. Same reasoning for \"america\" (mg vs rn — no hint; disambiguate). \n\nAlso \"vitoria da conquista\" hint ba — fine. \"nacional\" hint am — hmm, bare \"Nacional\" — Cup has \"Nacional - AM\"; Libertadores Nacionals have country tags. So registry states for bare base \"nacional\": {am} → auto-inherits am — hint redundant but harmless. Actually wait — is that right? Any bare \"Nacional\" in the data? Cup: \"Nacional - AM\" suffixed. So no bare → hint never fires at registration. Query \"Nacional\" → base nacional, stateless → registry entities with base \"nacional\": \"nacional am\" (from Cup), \"nacional uru\", \"nacional par\" (foreign