{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788251669039,"sessionID":"ses_fa40f0460ffeYpKGnJPSwDwXVy","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"edit","callID":"call_29b0991978c84e1a837d85b0","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-7ooj92cj/retort-e48763b54868/soccer_mcp/normalize.py","oldString":"#: Explicit folds for clubs whose *base* itself changes between sources.\n#: Keys are ``(base, state_or_None)``; a ``None`` key state means \"bare\n#: spelling only\".  Values are the canonical ``(base, state)``.\nALIASES: dict[tuple[str, str | None], tuple[str, str | None]] = {","newString":"#: Alias keys that must only fire for *bare* spellings (the base also exists\n#: with a different state for another club, so a suffixed spelling must not be\n#: hijacked).\nBARE_ONLY_ALIASES = {(\"bragantino\", None)}\n\n#: Explicit folds for clubs whose *base* itself changes between sources.\n#: Keys are ``(base, state_or_None)``.  A ``None`` key state means the rename\n#: applies to any spelling of that base (bare or state-suffixed), unless the\n#: key is listed in ``BARE_ONLY_ALIASES``.  Exact ``(base, state)`` keys are\n#: state-specific merges.  Values are the canonical ``(base, state)``.\nALIASES: dict[tuple[str, str | None], tuple[str, str | None]] = {"},"output":"Edit applied successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"diff":"Index: /Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-7ooj92cj/retort-e48763b54868/soccer_mcp/normalize.py\n===================================================================\n--- /Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-7ooj92cj/retort-e48763b54868/soccer_mcp/normalize.py\n+++ /Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-7ooj92cj/retort-e48763b54868/soccer_mcp/normalize.py\n@@ -200,11 +200,18 @@\n \n     return ParsedTeamName(base=_clean_base(text), state=state, country=country)\n \n \n+#: Alias keys that must only fire for *bare* spellings (the base also exists\n+#: with a different state for another club, so a suffixed spelling must not be\n+#: hijacked).\n+BARE_ONLY_ALIASES = {(\"bragantino\", None)}\n+\n #: Explicit folds for clubs whose *base* itself changes between sources.\n-#: Keys are ``(base, state_or_None)``; a ``None`` key state means \"bare\n-#: spelling only\".  Values are the canonical ``(base, state)``.\n+#: Keys are ``(base, state_or_None)``.  A ``None`` key state means the rename\n+#: applies to any spelling of that base (bare or state-suffixed), unless the\n+#: key is listed in ``BARE_ONLY_ALIASES``.  Exact ``(base, state)`` keys are\n+#: state-specific merges.  Values are the canonical ``(base, state)``.\n ALIASES: dict[tuple[str, str | None], tuple[str, str | None]] = {\n     # -- Atlético family -----------------------------------------------------\n     (\"atletico mineiro\", None): (\"atletico\", \"mg\"),\n     (\"atletico goianiense\", None): (\"atletico\", \"go\"),\n","filediff":{"file":"/Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-7ooj92cj/retort-e48763b54868/soccer_mcp/normalize.py","patch":"Index: /Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-7ooj92cj/retort-e48763b54868/soccer_mcp/normalize.py\n===================================================================\n--- /Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-7ooj92cj/retort-e48763b54868/soccer_mcp/normalize.py\n+++ /Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-7ooj92cj/retort-e48763b54868/soccer_mcp/normalize.py\n@@ -200,11 +200,18 @@\n \n     return ParsedTeamName(base=_clean_base(text), state=state, country=country)\n \n \n+#: Alias keys that must only fire for *bare* spellings (the base also exists\n+#: with a different state for another club, so a suffixed spelling must not be\n+#: hijacked).\n+BARE_ONLY_ALIASES = {(\"bragantino\", None)}\n+\n #: Explicit folds for clubs whose *base* itself changes between sources.\n-#: Keys are ``(base, state_or_None)``; a ``None`` key state means \"bare\n-#: spelling only\".  Values are the canonical ``(base, state)``.\n+#: Keys are ``(base, state_or_None)``.  A ``None`` key state means the rename\n+#: applies to any spelling of that base (bare or state-suffixed), unless the\n+#: key is listed in ``BARE_ONLY_ALIASES``.  Exact ``(base, state)`` keys are\n+#: state-specific merges.  Values are the canonical ``(base, state)``.\n ALIASES: dict[tuple[str, str | None], tuple[str, str | None]] = {\n     # -- Atlético family -----------------------------------------------------\n     (\"atletico mineiro\", None): (\"atletico\", \"mg\"),\n     (\"atletico goianiense\", None): (\"atletico\", \"go\"),\n","additions":9,"deletions":2},"truncated":false},"title":"soccer_mcp/normalize.py","time":{"start":1788251669034,"end":1788251669037}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Wait — there's an issue with the `_count_skipped` for the cup file: rows with NA goals are skipped in `_load_cup` via `registry.observe()` but NOT registered (`registry.register` is not called) — so those teams don't get counted for that season via note_match, but more importantly, the teams in NA rows won't be registered with variants. That's OK-ish, but those clubs' variants from other rows cover it. Hmm — actually wait, there's a subtler issue: `registry.observe()` in pass 0 already covers state observation for ALL rows including NA rows. Fine.\n\nBut there IS a real problem in `_load_cup`: I call `registry.observe` for NA rows but never `register` — that means e.g. if a club ONLY appears in NA rows it wouldn't be an entity. Not the case here (Fluminense, Grêmio etc. appear elsewhere). Fine.\n\nAnother issue: `_count_skipped` reads CSVs again — a bit wasteful but clean and only at load time. OK.\n\nAlso `date.min` needs importing `date` in data_loader — I used `date.min` in `_match_sort_key` but imported only `dataclass, field, lru_cache, Path`... I need `from datetime import date`. Let me fix.\n\nAlso `vars(counters)` returns dict with keys rows/skipped/skipped_examples ✓.\n\nWait — one more issue in `source_priority_for`: `_source_skipped_counts` for SRC_CUP looks up `quality['skipped_by_comp_season']` keyed by season string — `_count_skipped` for cup groups by `row['season']` ✓. For libertadores, keyed by season — but the NA row has season 'NA' which is skipped by the `if season.upper() != \"NA\"` check → libertadores 2022's skipped count is 0 (the NA row is season 'NA' not '2022')... and 2021 final simply doesn't exist → no skip recorded → fine, source priority unaffected (only one source anyway).\n\nHmm, but wait — there's a subtle issue with the Libertadores NA row: home_team 'Flamengo', away 'Athletico' — observed in pass 0 but row skipped in load. OK.\n\nAlso in `_load_cup` for NA rows — I do `registry.observe` (pass-0 style) — harmless.\n\nNow — a significant design issue in `iter_matches`: when `competition` or `season` is None, `keys = list(self._by_comp_season)` — ALL comps/seasons → each with its default source → good. When `source` param given and `competition=None`: for each key, chosen = source if present. ✓.\n\n`_match_sort_key` uses `match.match_date or date.min` — matches with None date sort first. All loaded matches have dates (I skip NA-dated rows? hmm — actually no: in `_load_libertadores` I skip rows with season NA or goals None — the NA row has datetime NA AND season NA → skipped ✓. But could a row have valid goals but NA datetime? Libertadores NA row is the only NA datetime. OK — but what about rows with None match_date that pass? `parse_date_any` returns None for bad dates; such matches would have date None and season \"\". For safety they're included (sorted first). Fine.\n\n`Serie A 2023` season determination for br_football uses date year ✓.\n\nOne more consideration — `note_match` increments match_count per match per team. For teams in multiple sources (Flamengo in brasileirao+cup+libertadores+br_football) — count is inflated by duplicates. It's only used for ranking alternatives/fuzzy — fine.\n\nWait, actually there's a REAL problem with double registration: pass 1 `_load_*` functions register teams per row. `_load_cup` NA rows observe only. But `TeamRegistry.register` uses `canonical_team_name(raw, registry_states=self._states_by_base)` — the states map was built in pass 0 from ALL files. \n\nBUT there's an ordering bug in pass 0 vs aliases: `observe()` applies `apply_aliases(parse_team_name(raw))`. For \"Atletico Mineiro\" (BR-Football bare) → alias → (\"atletico\",\"mg\") → observes base \"atletico\" state mg. ✓ Good — aliases applied before observing.\n\nHmm, but \"Vasco\" → alias → (\"vasco da gama\", None) → no state observed. And \"Vasco da Gama - RJ\" (cup) → (\"vasco da gama\",\"rj\") → observed ✓ → bare \"Vasco\" inherits rj ✓.\n\nNow — critical check — `register()` for bare names: `canonical_team_name(raw, registry_states=...)`. E.g. bare \"Corinthians\" (novo): base \"corinthians\", states observed {sp} → inherits sp ✓. Bare \"Santos\" (BR-Football/Libertadores/FIFA): states {sp, ap} → multiple → FAMOUS_STATE_HINTS[\"santos\"]=\"sp\" → \"santos sp\" ✓. Bare \"Botafogo\" → {rj, pb, sp} → hint rj ✓. Bare \"Flamengo\" → {rj, pi} → hint rj ✓. Bare \"Internacional\" → {rs, sc} → hint rs ✓. Bare \"America\"? no bare América exists... EXCEPT — wait: BR-Football has 'America MG' and 'America RN' (state in base) — parse extracts state from trailing \"MG\"/\"RN\" ✓ → (\"america\", mg/rn) ✓. \n\nHmm — BUT what about \"America FC Natal\"? parse: trailing \"Natal\" not a state → base \"america fc natal\" → alias → (\"america\",\"rn\") ✓.\n\nNow a NEW worry — `parse_team_name` trailing-state regex on BR-Football bases like \"Bahia de Feira\" — ends \"Feira\" ✓ no state. \"Botafogo PB\" ✓ pb. \"River AC\" → (\"river\",\"ac\")... hmm! \"River AC\" — BR-Football — is that River-AC?? Wait, Cup has \"Ríver - PI\". BR-Football \"River AC\"... Hmm, is there a River-AC? I don't think so! \"River AC\" might be a data error for Ríver-PI? Or actually — hmm. There might genuinely be a \"Rio Branco\"... no. Let me think: BR-Football Serie C 2014-2023 — \"River AC\"?? I'm not sure. There could be an obscure club. I'll leave it — if it's wrong, it's a tiny data quirk. Actually wait — I recall the Copão... no. Leave it.\n\n\"Juventude MA\" ✓ distinct from Juventude-RS. \"Nautico RR\" ✓.\n\nAlso \"Santa Cruz FC\" → alias → (\"santa cruz\",\"pe\") ✓ — states observed for base \"santa cruz\": {pe, rn, rs} → multiple → hint pe ✓ consistent.\n\n\"Santa Cruz RN\"/\"RS\" → direct states ✓.\n\nNow — \"Gremio\" bare → states observed for base \"gremio\": from \"Grêmio - RS\" → rs; from \"Grêmio RS\" → rs. So {rs} → inherits rs ✓. But WAIT — \"Gremio Anapolis\" → base \"gremio anapolis\" (different base) ✓. \"Gremio Novorizontino\" → alias → (\"novorizontino\",\"sp\") — observes base \"novorizontino\" sp ✓. \"Grêmio Barueri\" → alias barueri sp ✓. \"Grêmio Prudente\" → alias barueri sp ✓.\n\n\"Cruzeiro\" bare → {mg} ✓. \"Ceara\" bare → {ce} ✓ (from \"Ceará - CE\" cup? hmm — Cup has \"Ceará - CE\" ✓). \"Fortaleza\" bare → {ce} ✓. \"Bahia\" bare → {ba} ✓. \"Vitoria\" bare → {ba, es} → hint ba ✓. \"Sport\" bare → {pe} ✓. \"Parana\" bare → {pr} ✓ (Cup \"Paraná - PR\" ✓). \"Goias\" bare → {go} ✓. \"Cuiaba\" bare → {mt} ✓ (BR \"Cuiaba MT\", Cup \"Cuiabá - MT\" ✓). \"Chapecoense\" → {sc} ✓. \"Avai\" → {sc} ✓. \"Criciuma\" → {sc} ✓. \"Figueirense\" → {sc} ✓. \"Coritiba\" → {pr} ✓. \"Ponte Preta\" → {sp} ✓. \"Portuguesa\" → {sp, rj} → hint sp ✓. \"Guarani\" → {sp, ce} → hint sp ✓. \"CSA\" → base \"csa\" — Cup \"Csa - AL\"/\"C.s.a.\" → {al} ✓. \"CRB\" → {al} ✓. \"ASA\" → {al} ✓. \"ABC\" → {rn} ✓. \"Remo\" → {pa} ✓. \"Paysandu\" → {pa} ✓. \"Nautico\" → {pe, rr} → hint pe ✓. \"Ipatinga\" → {mg}? Cup doesn't have Ipatinga... novo bare \"Ipatinga\" — states observed for \"ipatinga\": NONE (only bare occurrence) → stays stateless \"ipatinga\". Hmm! That breaks the ideal \"ipatinga mg\" — but does it matter? Ipatinga only appears in novo (Serie A 2008-2012?) — single source, no merge needed. Stateless id is fine. ✓ (my hint table has \"ipatinga\": \"mg\" — wait, did I keep it? In the rewrite I removed most single-state hints. Let me check... In the final FAMOUS_STATE_HINTS I only kept: flamengo, fluminense, botafogo, santos, internacional, juventude, vitoria, nautico, santa cruz, guarani, portuguesa. So \"ipatinga\" stays stateless — fine.)\n\nHmm wait — but hold on: if \"Ipatinga\" is stateless id \"ipatinga\", then a user query \"Ipatinga\" → resolve: parsed base \"ipatinga\" stateless → `_by_base(\"ipatinga\")` → 1 entity → found ✓. Good.\n\n\"Barueri\" bare (novo 2009) → states for \"barueri\": Cup \"Grêmio Barueri - SP\" → alias → (\"barueri\",\"sp\") observed ✓ → inherits sp ✓.\n\"Grêmio Prudente\" (novo 2010) → alias barueri+sp ✓ SAME id as Barueri ✓ nice.\n\"Brasiliense\" → {df} ✓ (Cup \"Brasiliense - DF\" ✓). \"Santo André\" → {sp} ✓ (Cup \"Santo André - SP\" ✓). \"São Caetano\" → {sp} ✓ (Cup ✓). \"Joinville\" → {sc} ✓ (Cup ✓). \"Sampaio Corrêa\" → {ma} ✓ (Cup ✓). \"Tuna Luso\"? novo doesn't have... it was in my old hints; removed — BR-Football 'Tuna Luso' bare → states for \"tuna luso\": none → stateless. fine.\n\nForeign: \"Boca Juniors\" stateless id \"boca juniors\" ✓. \"Flamengo\" in Libertadores → (\"flamengo\", None) → hint rj ✓ (since {rj,pi} multiple). Wait — hint fires ✓.\n\n\"Athletico\" (Libertadores) → alias → (\"atletico\",\"pr\") ✓.\n\n\"Barcelona-EQU\" → country equ → id \"barcelona equ\" ✓. \"Nacional (URU)\" → \"nacional uru\" ✓. \"Nacional-URU\" → \"nacional uru\" ✓. \"Guaraní (PAR)\" → \"guarani par\" ✓ — note: this is FOREIGN \"guarani par\" vs Brazilian \"guarani sp\" — distinct ids ✓. But careful: query \"Guarani\" → parsed base \"guarani\" stateless → `_by_base(\"guarani\")` → [\"guarani sp\", \"guarani ce\", \"guarani par\"(foreign)] → brazilian = [guarani sp, guarani ce] → 2 → ambiguous → hint? FAMOUS_STATE_HINTS[\"guarani\"]=\"sp\" — WAIT, the hint is applied inside `canonical_team_name` BEFORE the candidates check! Let me re-trace resolve(): `parsed = canonical_team_name(query, registry_states=...)` — for \"Guarani\": states {sp, ce} → multiple → hint sp → parsed = (\"guarani\",\"sp\") → since parsed.state → entity = entities.get(\"guarani sp\") → found ✓ with alternatives [guarani ce, guarani par]. \n\nAnd for \"Atletico\": states {mg, go, pr, ba, ac} → multiple → no hint → stateless parsed → `_by_base(\"atletico\")` → [atletico mg, atletico go, atletico pr, atletico ba, atletico ac, atletico cearense ce] — all brazilian → ambiguous error listing them ✓ (with most matches first).\n\nQuery \"Atletico-MG\" → parse → (\"atletico\",\"mg\") → exact ✓.\nQuery \"Athletico-PR\" → parse → (\"athletico\",\"pr\")... WAIT — parse gives base \"athletico\" state \"pr\" → then `canonical_team_name` applies alias ([\"athletico\", None]... the lookup: `ALIASES.get((\"athletico\",\"pr\"))` → None → then since state is NOT None, the None-key fallback does NOT fire → parsed stays (\"athletico\",\"pr\") → team_id \"athletico pr\" → entities.get(\"athletico pr\") → NOT FOUND (registered as \"atletico pr\")! BUG!\n\nThe alias table has (\"athletico\", None) → (\"atletico\", \"pr\") which only fires for BARE \"Athletico\". For \"Athletico-PR\" I need (\"athletico\", \"pr\") → (\"atletico\",\"pr\") too. Hmm — my `apply_aliases` restriction \"None key = bare only\" breaks state-suffixed variant spellings of renamed bases.\n\nFix: for ALIASES entries where the target state is explicitly forced (like athletico→pr), the (base, None) entry should apply regardless of parsed state — because \"Athletico\" only ever means Athletico-PR. But for entries like (\"bragantino\", None) → (\"red bull bragantino\",\"sp\") — that must NOT catch (\"bragantino\",\"pa\").\n\nResolution: add explicit entries: (\"athletico\", \"pr\") → (\"atletico\",\"pr\"). And generally, rename-style aliases (base-rename where source base ≠ target base and there's no state ambiguity) should apply for any state. Let me categorize: entries like (\"atletico mineiro\", None)→(\"atletico\",\"mg\") — \"Atletico Mineiro-XX\" with XX≠MG doesn't exist; applying for any state is safe. (\"gremio novorizontino\", None) — safe any state. (\"bragantino\", None) — NOT safe (PA exists). (\"ferroviario\", None)→ce — \"Ferroviario-XX\" XX≠CE? Cup \"Ferroviário - CE\" only. Safe. (\"uniao\",\"mt\") exact ✓. (\"brasil\",\"rs\") exact ✓. (\"anapolis\",\"go\") exact ✓. (\"vitoria fc\",\"es\") exact ✓. (\"america fc\",\"mg\") exact ✓. (\"parnahyba sc\",\"pi\") exact ✓. (\"operario fc\",\"ms\") exact ✓. (\"sfrancisco\",\"pa\") exact ✓. (\"aguia\",\"pa\") exact ✓. (\"tolima\",None) safe. (\"cs alagoano\",None) safe. (\"sete de setembro\",None)→(\"7 de setembro\",\"ms\") — safe. \n\nThe DANGEROUS ones (must stay bare-only or exact): (\"bragantino\", None), (\"athletico\", None)→ but that's the whole point of the entry... \n\nCleanest fix: make None-key entries apply to ANY state EXCEPT when the alias would CHANGE an existing distinct entity's state — too clever. Alternative: add explicit (\"athletico\", \"pr\") entry AND keep (\"athletico\", None) for bare. And for other renames, keep None=bare-only but add explicit state entries where the suffixed spelling occurs in data:\n- \"Athletico-PR\" appears in: novo (2019: \"Athletico-PR\"), Cup (\"Athletico Paranaense - PR\" — different base, fine), Libertadores (\"Athletico\" bare). So add (\"athletico\",\"pr\").\n- Are there other base-rename spellings WITH state suffix? \"Atletico Paranaense - PR\" → base \"atletico paranaense\" + pr → alias None-key... state is pr → None-key does NOT fire! BUG again! (\"atletico paranaense\", None) only fires bare. But Cup has \"Atlético Paranaense - PR\" and BR-Football has bare \"Atletico Paranaense\"/\"Athletico Paranaense\". So I need (\"atletico paranaense\",\"pr\") → (\"atletico\",\"pr\") and (\"athletico paranaense\",\"pr\") too.\n\nHmm — this is getting tedious but let me systematically decide: rename aliases SHOULD apply for any state (they're base renames, not state-dependent merges), EXCEPT \"bragantino\" (state