tition season. For Serie A that's usually aligned (season = calendar year), except postponed matches (2020 season ended Feb 2021! The 2020 Brasileirão final rounds were played in Jan-Feb 2021 due to COVID). So br_football \"2020 season\" matches played in 2021 count under 2021 → Serie A 2020: fewer, 2021: more.\n\n   Impact: when serving serie_a 2021 from br_football (not default — brasileirao 2021 is complete → default is brasileirao ✓) it doesn't matter for defaults. But if the user queries source=br_football, the season split is slightly off. It's a data limitation; document it. But ALSO — standings from br_football 2021 (491 matches) would be wrong. Default is brasileirao → fine.\n\n   BUT serie_a 2022 → br_football (379) ✓ good. serie_a 2023 → br_football (only source) — 2023 season by date = calendar 2023 ✓ mostly fine (2023 season fully within 2023).\n\n   And serie_a 2016 → brasileirao skipped=1 → falls to novo (380 rows, includes the cancelled match as 0-0 draw — factually wrong but that's the dataset's choice). Hmm — alternatively I could treat \"1 skipped row out of 380\" as acceptable completeness (379 playable). The problem: my rule \"prefer sources with 0 skipped\" is too strict — a single cancelled match makes the entire dedicated file lose priority. Better rule: prefer source with skipped==0, BUT allow small tolerances? Or prefer the source with MORE playable matches for that season? Cleaner rule: **choose the source with the most playable matches for the (comp, season)**, breaking ties by priority order. Let me check implications:\n   - serie_a 2016: brasileirao 379 vs novo 380 vs br_football (2016 by date... some 2015-postponed or 2016 season spillover?) — probably ~380. novo wins (380) → novo 2016 (includes fake 0-0). Hmm. Or brasileirao 379 (excludes cancelled — more truthful!). \n   \n   Hmm, tricky. Which is \"right\"? The cancelled match was never played; novo recorded it as 0-0 (they filled it). Truth-wise, brasileirao (379) is more accurate. But by \"most playable matches\" rule, novo wins.\n\n   Alternative rule: **priority order, but skip a source if its playable count for the season is less than 90% of the best source's count**. For 2016: brasileirao 379 vs best 380 → 99.7% ≥ 90% → brasileirao stays (priority 1) ✓. For 2022: brasileirao 299 vs best 379 → 79% < 90% → fall to br_football ✓. For copa 2021: cup file playable count? 108 - 16 = 92 vs br_football 111 → 92/111 = 83% < 90% → br_football ✓. For copa 2012-2013: only cup file ✓. For copa 2014-2020: cup file vs br_football — cup playable ~120 vs br_football ~110? Let me check br_football copa counts per year: 2021: 111, 2022: 117, 2023: 116. Hmm what about 2014-2020? Need counts. If cup file has 120 and br_football has fewer for those years, cup wins anyway by priority (as long as ≥90%).\n\n   Hmm wait — but there's a subtlety: for copa 2016, cup file max round 7 — maybe cup file 2016 is itself missing the final? 162 matches in 2016 vs 120 in 2017-2020... 2016: 162?! That's odd — 2016 had MORE matches recorded. Real Copa do Brasil 2016: 91 matches. 162?? Maybe the 2016 data includes first-round pairs... whatever, Kaggle data quirks. br_football 2016 count unknown. Let me compute both quickly and decide.\n\n   Actually — let me simplify the rule to: **among available sources for (comp, season), pick the one with the most playable matches; tie-break by priority order**. Check:\n   - serie_a 2012-2021: brasileirao 380 vs novo 380 (2012-2019) vs br_football (variable). Tie 380-380 → priority: brasileirao ✓.\n   - serie_a 2003-2011: novo only ✓.\n   - serie_a 2016: novo 380 vs brasileirao 379 → NOVO wins (380>379). Hmm — I'd prefer brasileirao... but honestly novo's 380 includes the never-played match as 0-0. The difference is negligible for demo purposes. Accept \"most playable wins\" — simple, predictable, and fixes the real problems (2022, copa 2021). Actually — hmm, wait. Let me reconsider: is it really better to use the file with the fabricated 0-0? The standings for 2016 would count a phantom draw. Chapecoense finished 2016 with... the CBF table has them with 37 games? no wait — officially the match wasn't played; CBF kept 37 matches for both teams? Hmm, actually in the official 2016 table Chapecoense played 37 and Atlético-MG 37? No — I believe the official CBF 2016 table shows all teams with 38 matches except Chapecoense and Atlético-MG with 37. Let me verify with standings from both sources... This is a demo; either is defensible. BUT for the \"Who won the 2016 Brasileirão?\" answer: Palmeiras won 2016 — both sources give that.\n\n   Decision: **most playable matches, tie-break by priority**. It's objective and robust. The 2016 phantom-draw edge case gets documented in README data-quality notes.\n\n   Hmm — one more check: copa 2016: cup file 162 playable? vs br_football 2016 copa count? If br_football has fewer, cup wins ✓ fine either way.\n\n   And serie_a 2021: brasileirao 380 vs br_football 491(!) → br_football WINS by count! DISASTER — 491 includes 2020-season postponed matches; standings from it would be garbage (double-round anomaly). So \"most playable\" is WRONG for serie_a 2021!\n\n   Ugh. br_football's date-based season attribution poisons the count rule. Options:\n   a) Keep priority-based rule but with a completeness tolerance: use priority order; skip a source only if its playable count < 90% of max across sources. serie_a 2021: brasileirao 380 ≥ 90%×491=442? NO — 380 < 442 → falls to br_football! Still wrong!\n   b) Cap consideration: exclude counts > expected? For a league, expected matches per season ≈ (n_teams choose 2)×2... unknown n_teams a priori. A season with 491 matches when the dedicated file has 380 is suspicious (29% more). Rule could be: skip source if its count deviates from the MEDIAN by >15%? Over-engineering.\n   c) Priority-first with \"completeness vs priority-1 source\": start with priority-1 source for the comp; if a lower-priority source has STRICTLY more playable matches AND the priority-1 source is missing >5% of that count, switch. serie_a 2021: brasileirao 380; br_football 491 > 380 and 380 < 95%×491=466 → switch to br_football. STILL wrong!\n\n   Hmm. The fundamental issue: br_football 2021's 491 is inflated by misattributed 2020-season matches. What if I fix the SEASON ATTRIBUTION for br_football instead? For Brazilian Serie A/B/C and Copa do Brasil, season = calendar year of date is ~correct except for COVID-era spillovers (2020→Jan/Feb 2021). The real 2020 Serie A season ran Aug 2020 - Feb 2021! So br_football 2020-season matches played in 2021 are attributed to 2021. True 2020 season: 380 matches; br_football shows (by date) 2020: ~300ish? and 2021: 491 (380 of 2021 + ~111 tail of 2020).\n\n   Precise fix: reassign br_football matches from Jan-Apr of year Y+1 to season Y (postponed-match spillover window). Brazilian seasons run Apr-Dec; spillover only happens Jan-Feb (2021). Rule: br_football match with date month ≤ 3 → season = year - 1. Check: does this hold generally? 2014-2023: Copa do Brasil finals in Sep-Nov; Serie A ends Dec; Serie C... any matches legitimately played Jan-Mar? 2023 Serie A started Apr ✓. 2014-2019 seasons Apr-Dec ✓. 2020 season: Aug 2020 → Feb 2021 → Jan-Feb 2021 matches reassigned to 2020 ✓. 2022 season: Apr-Dec 2022 ✓. So rule: month ≤ 3 → previous year. \n\n   With that fix: br_football 2021 → ~380-ish (491 - spillover). Let me verify empirically. Then \"most playable, tie-break priority\" works:\n   - serie_a 2021: brasileirao 380 = br_football ~380 → tie → brasileirao ✓\n   - serie_a 2020: brasileirao 380 vs br_football (~300 + spillover back ≈ 380) ✓ tie → brasileirao\n   - serie_a 2022: brasileirao 299 vs br_football 379 → br_football ✓\n   - serie_a 2016: novo 380 > brasileirao 379 → novo (phantom draw — acceptable, documented)\n   - copa 2021: cup 92 < br_football 111 → br_football ✓\n   - copa 2014-2020: cup file vs br_football — need counts; if cup file ≥ br_football → cup ✓ else switch (also fine).\n   - serie_b/c: br_football only ✓.\n   - libertadores: single ✓.\n\n   Let me verify the br_football season counts after the Jan-Mar reassignment rule. Quick check now.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05c1fc543001n2nbR656FojjHR","sessionID":"ses_fa40f0460ffeYpKGnJPSwDwXVy","messageID":"msg_05c1e4e8a001aRYBuQdsF24v9F"}}
