{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788252437669,"sessionID":"ses_fa40f0460ffeYpKGnJPSwDwXVy","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"write","callID":"call_3912c377475e49f182630498","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"content":"\"\"\"\nsoccer_mcp.queries -- the analytical query layer.\n\nCONTEXT\n-------\nThe MCP tools (``soccer_mcp.tools``) answer the natural-language question\ncategories required by the Brazilian Soccer MCP specification:\n\n* Match queries    -- find matches by team, opponent, date range, competition,\n                      season, stage (\"Flamengo vs Fluminense\", \"Palmeiras\n                      matches in 2023\", \"Copa do Brasil finals\");\n* Team queries     -- records, home/away splits, head-to-head, comparisons,\n                      competitions a team appeared in;\n* Player queries   -- name/nationality/club/position/rating filters over the\n                      FIFA database, including cross-file club matching;\n* Competition queries -- computed standings, champions, finals and knockout\n                      brackets;\n* Statistics       -- average goals, home/away win rates, biggest wins,\n                      derby fixtures.\n\nEvery function here is a pure function of a loaded ``SoccerData`` and returns\nplain Python structures, so the whole layer is testable without MCP.  The\n``tools`` module adds name resolution and text formatting on top.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass, field\n\nfrom .data_loader import SoccerData\nfrom .model import (\n    FinalResult,\n    KnockoutTie,\n    Match,\n    Player,\n    StandingRow,\n    TeamEntity,\n    TeamRecord,\n    TeamResolution,\n)\nfrom .normalize import (\n    COMPETITIONS,\n    SKILL_ALIASES if False else None,  # placeholder removed below\n)\n\n# NOTE: SKILL_ALIASES does not exist; real imports follow.\n","filePath":"/Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-7ooj92cj/retort-e48763b54868/soccer_mcp/queries.py"},"output":"Wrote file successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"filepath":"/Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-7ooj92cj/retort-e48763b54868/soccer_mcp/queries.py","exists":false,"truncated":false},"title":"soccer_mcp/queries.py","time":{"start":1788252437665,"end":1788252437668}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Source selection is now fully correct:\n- serie_a: novo (2003-2011), brasileirao (2012-2021), br_football (2022-2023) ✓\n- copa_do_brasil: cup file (2012-2020), br_football (2021-2023) ✓\n- Copa 2021 final present: Atlético-MG vs Athletico (aggregate 6-1 AM) ✓\n\nNow write the queries layer. This is the heart of the tool surface. Let me carefully design each function:\n\n```python\n# queries.py\n- resolve_team(ds, name) -> TeamResolution\n- search_matches(ds, team=None, opponent=None, competition=None, season=None, stage=None, date_from=None, date_to=None, source=None, limit=20) -> dict(result=matches, total, team_display, ...)\n- head_to_head(ds, team_a, team_b, competition=None, season=None) -> dict\n- team_stats(ds, team, competition=None, season=None, venue=None) -> dict(overall, home, away records)\n- compare_teams(ds, a, b, ...) -> dict\n- team_competitions(ds, team) -> dict\n- last_match(ds, team, opponent=None) -> Match|None\n- search_players(ds, name=None, nationality=None, club=None, position=None, min_overall=None, max_age=None, limit=20) -> list[Player]\n- find_player(ds, name) -> list[Player]\n- top_players(ds, nationality=None, club=None, position=None, attribute=\"overall\", limit=10) -> list[Player]\n- standings(ds, competition, season) -> dict(rows=StandingRow list, competition, season, source, notes)\n- champion(ds, competition, season) -> dict\n- finals(ds, competition, season=None) -> list[FinalResult]\n- knockout(ds, competition, season) -> dict(stages -> ties)\n- biggest_wins(ds, competition=None, season=None, team=None, limit=10) -> list[Match]\n- competition_stats(ds, competition=None, season=None, team=None) -> dict\n- derbies(ds, season=None, competition=None) -> list[(label, Match)]\n- list_competitions(ds) -> list[CompetitionCoverage]\n- list_teams(ds, competition=None, season=None) -> list[TeamEntity]\n```\n\nKey algorithms:\n\n**standings**: league only. From default-source matches: TeamRecord per team → rows sorted by points/wins/gd/gf → positions. Notes: relegated = last 4 (for seasons with ≥12 teams; for 2003-2005 with 24 teams, still last 4 — historical relegation counts differed, note caveat). Champion = row 1.\n\n**finals**:\n- libertadores: stage=='final' matches per season; tie = the pair. 2013: two legs; 2020: single. tie.agg decides winner; if 0 → penalties note.\n- copa_do_brasil: per season on default source: if round labels exist (cup file \"Round N\"): final = matches with max N. For br_football seasons (no round): sort by date; last two — if same pair → two-legged final; else last one only.\n- final result aggregated.\n\n**knockout (bracket)**: libertadores: stages 'round of 16', 'quarterfinals', 'semifinals', 'final' → group matches by pair → ties with agg. copa_do_brasil: if rounds exist, group by round; each round's matches (two legs per pair mostly) → ties. For br_football copa seasons without rounds — can't group; return flat list note.\n\nHmm — for copa knockout with round labels: \"Round 8\" = final. Earlier rounds: two-legged? Most CdB rounds are two-legged except first round (single). The tie grouping by pair within round handles both (1 or 2 matches).\n\n**champion**: league → standings[0]; cup → finals season → winner; None → note.\n\n**biggest_wins**: filter matches; sort by (-margin, -total_goals, date); limit.\n\n**competition_stats**: matches (default sources, filters): count, avg total goals, home wins %, draws %, away wins %, avg home goals, avg away goals, biggest win ref.\n\n**derbies**: known pairs (canonical ids); iterate matches where pair matches; label. Include pairs where BOTH teams are in dataset. Filter season/comp.\n\n**last_match**: max date over team's matches.\n\nPlayer queries: straightforward filtering; club resolution via registry (resolve → team entity → fifa_club_names set); if club not resolvable as team but exists as raw FIFA club substring? The registry registers ALL FIFA clubs, so resolve(\"Juventus\") → entity \"juventus\" with fifa_club_names {\"Juventus\"} ✓. resolve(\"FC Barcelona\") → parse base \"fc barcelona\" stateless → entity ✓. Nationality: text_key match on nationality, or substring. Position: accept code (\"ST\") or group (\"FWD\").\n\ntop_players attribute: \"overall\"|\"potential\" or any skill column name (case-insensitive match).\n\nLet me also handle 'stage' filter mapping for Libertadores knockouts: stage param matches round_label or stage field substring ✓ already in iter_matches.\n\nFor `search_matches` result dict: {matches: [...], total: N, shown: M, resolution notes...} — formatting handles the rest.\n\nNow — resolution error handling in queries: each team-taking query function should return an error dict when resolution fails (not raise) so tools can format a helpful message. I'll design: query functions raise `QueryError(message, alternatives)` — a small exception; tools catch and format. Cleaner. Actually — for the query layer to be testable, returning structured results with error info is simpler. Hmm. Let me use exceptions for control flow (QueryError with `resolution` attached), and tools catch them. Tests can either catch or use resolve directly. OK.\n\nAlso `TeamRecord` sub-bundles for team_stats with venue split. And per-competition breakdown: team_stats returns list of per-comp records too.\n\nLet me write queries.py now.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05c26bca0001ngNC4y9LeoPECl","sessionID":"ses_fa40f0460ffeYpKGnJPSwDwXVy","messageID":"msg_05c25de31001bvhWxHbYs2VY0k"}}
