{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788253034409,"sessionID":"ses_fa40f0460ffeYpKGnJPSwDwXVy","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"edit","callID":"call_d3d4ae582b264ffd88429743","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-7ooj92cj/retort-e48763b54868/soccer_mcp/bdd.py","newString":"from __future__ import annotations\n\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\nfrom typing import Any, Callable","oldString":"from __future__ import annotations\n\nimport traceback\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass, field\nfrom typing import Any, Callable"},"output":"Edit applied successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"diff":"Index: /Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-7ooj92cj/retort-e48763b54868/soccer_mcp/bdd.py\n===================================================================\n--- /Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-7ooj92cj/retort-e48763b54868/soccer_mcp/bdd.py\n+++ /Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-7ooj92cj/retort-e48763b54868/soccer_mcp/bdd.py\n@@ -32,10 +32,9 @@\n \"\"\"\n \n from __future__ import annotations\n \n-import traceback\n-from dataclasses import dataclass, field\n+from dataclasses import dataclass\n from typing import Any, Callable\n \n \n @dataclass\n","filediff":{"file":"/Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-7ooj92cj/retort-e48763b54868/soccer_mcp/bdd.py","patch":"Index: /Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-7ooj92cj/retort-e48763b54868/soccer_mcp/bdd.py\n===================================================================\n--- /Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-7ooj92cj/retort-e48763b54868/soccer_mcp/bdd.py\n+++ /Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-7ooj92cj/retort-e48763b54868/soccer_mcp/bdd.py\n@@ -32,10 +32,9 @@\n \"\"\"\n \n from __future__ import annotations\n \n-import traceback\n-from dataclasses import dataclass, field\n+from dataclasses import dataclass\n from typing import Any, Callable\n \n \n @dataclass\n","additions":1,"deletions":2},"truncated":false},"title":"soccer_mcp/bdd.py","time":{"start":1788253034406,"end":1788253034408}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Wait — there's a bug in `when()`: \n```python\nfor key, action in actions.items():\n    self._steps.append(_Step(\"When\" if key == list(actions)[0] else \"And\", ...))\n```\n`list(actions)[0]` gives the first key — comparing key to first key to decide \"When\" vs \"And\". OK that works. But `Step.action` for Given: I append the step then set action. Fine.\n\nAlso in `run()`: for \"Given\" steps — `_Step(\"Given\", ...)` with stores=None — action updates context ✓. If a Given fails (e.g. exception) → since kind is \"Given\" → raise AssertionError with trace ✓. But note: `step.kind in {\"When\", \"And\"}` captures exceptions into ctx[\"error\"] — but for a Given failure we raise. Hmm — but what about When steps that legitimately fail (query raises)? Captured ✓ so Then can assert `ctx[\"error\"]` is not None. \n\nOne catch: a `then` after a captured When-error: assertion callables usually access ctx[\"result\"] which would be None → they'd fail with confusing messages. Test authors handle it. Fine.\n\nAlso — `traceback` import unused in bdd.py. Remove it.\n\nNow write the tests. conftest.py with session-scoped dataset fixture. Then test files:\n\n1. tests/test_normalize.py — BDD scenarios for normalization:\n   - state suffixes, bare names + hints, dot abbreviations, parenthetical, foreign country tags, alias renames, date parsing, competition normalization, position groups.\n\n2. tests/test_match_queries.py — BDD per the spec's Gherkin:\n   - Find matches between two teams (spec scenario 1)\n   - matches by team+season (\"What matches did Palmeiras play in 2023?\")\n   - by competition\n   - by date range\n   - by stage (finals)\n   - dedup check: same fixture not repeated (serie a 2019 has exactly 380 in dedup default)\n   - last match between teams\n   - error case: unknown team returns helpful error\n\n3. tests/test_team_queries.py —\n   - team stats Palmeiras 2023 (spec scenario 2: statistics for \"Palmeiras\" season 2023)\n   - Corinthians home record 2022\n   - head_to_head Palmeiras vs Santos\n   - compare_teams\n   - best away record\n   - team stats error/ambiguity\n\n4. tests/test_player_queries.py —\n   - find Gabriel Barbosa\n   - Brazilian players top\n   - players at a Brazilian club (Santos)\n   - forwards from São Paulo FC\n   - min_overall filter\n   - FIFA coverage note for Flamengo\n\n5. tests/test_competition_queries.py —\n   - standings 2019 Serie A: Flamengo champion 90 pts (assert top row)\n   - relegated 2020 (Chapecoense? real 2020 relegated: Vasco, Goiás, Coritiba, Botafogo — assert those 4)\n   - champion Libertadores 2019 (Flamengo)\n   - champion Copa do Brasil 2019 (Athletico-PR), 2021 (Atlético-MG), 2022 (penalties note), 2023 (São Paulo)\n   - finals all seasons (Copas do Brasil finals list)\n   - knockout bracket Libertadores 2018\n   - champion serie B 2023? real: Santos? no wait — 2023 Série B champion: Santos? no — 2023 Série B was won by... Santos was relegated in 2023 from Serie A and played 2024 Série B! 2023 Série B champion: ??? Hmm — I don't remember (Criciúma? They got promoted 2023... Criciúma won Série B 2023? I think yes — Criciúma was 2023 Série B champion? Actually I recall Criciúma got promoted. Champion... let me not assert specifics I'm unsure of; instead assert structural properties (20 teams, top has most points) or compute from data and verify consistency with the matches (self-consistency). For Série B, assert table has 20 teams and champion points > 70.\n\nActually for relegated 2020 Serie A: real: Vasco da Gama, Goiás, Coritiba, Botafogo ✓ (2020 relegated 4). Data should reproduce.\n\n2019 Serie A champion Flamengo 90 pts ✓ verified earlier.\n\n6. tests/test_statistics.py —\n   - average goals Brasileirão ~2.4-2.6 (assert 2.0 < avg < 3.0)\n   - home win rate > away win rate (structural)\n   - biggest wins Libertadores (Santos 8-0 Bolívar? hmm — earlier I saw \"2012-05-27: Santos 8-0 Bolivar\" in spec — wait is that in our data? Libertadores file covers 2013+. 2012 not included! The spec example \"1. 2012-05-27: Santos 8-0 Bolivar (Libertadores)\" — from BR-Football? No — BR-Football starts 2014. So the 2012 Libertadores isn't in the data. The spec example is aspirational. In OUR data, biggest Libertadores win: let me compute at test time and assert the top margin ≥ 6 and is correctly ordered. I'll write the test to check ordering + margin ≥ 5 without hard-coding.\n   - derbies 2023 (Fla-Flu etc. present)\n   - Grenal exists\n\n7. tests/test_mcp_server.py — in-memory MCP client: list tools (20 tools), call search_matches, standings, champion, search_players; assert is_error False and content non-empty. Plus stdio subprocess smoke test (list tools + one call) with generous timeout.\n\n8. tests/test_sample_questions.py — the ≥20 sample questions parametrized, each mapped to a tool call; assert non-empty, non-error response.\n\n9. tests/test_performance.py — timing marks: simple lookups < 2s, aggregates < 5s (on cached dataset; these will be ms in practice).\n\nconftest.py: session fixture `dataset` (loads once), `scenario` helper maybe not needed (import from soccer_mcp.bdd). Ensure repo root on sys.path for imports (tests run from repo root; pytest adds rootdir? With no installed package, need sys.path — pytest automatically inserts rootdir into sys.path when using rootdir conftest? Actually pytest inserts the conftest's parent dir (rootdir) into sys.path with \"rootdir\" conftest collection under \"prepend\" import mode if there's no __init__.py in tests/. I'll create tests/ without __init__.py and rely on conftest sys.path insertion. Simplest: in conftest.py do sys.path.insert(0, repo_root). \n\nLet me now write all test files. First remove the unused traceback import from bdd.py.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05c2fd79c0010XviCIfb5w6vAr","sessionID":"ses_fa40f0460ffeYpKGnJPSwDwXVy","messageID":"msg_05c2f0293001wa61qw