{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788253561976,"sessionID":"ses_fa40f0460ffeYpKGnJPSwDwXVy","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_12a94368e28542baaa1ef884","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"source venv/bin/activate && python << 'EOF'\nimport csv\nfrom soccer_mcp.normalize import parse_team_name, apply_aliases\n\n# bare names in Libertadores (no state, no country)\nlib_bare = set()\nwith open('data/kaggle/Libertadores_Matches.csv', encoding='utf-8') as f:\n    for row in csv.DictReader(f):\n        for t in (row['home_team'], row['away_team']):\n            p = apply_aliases(parse_team_name(t))\n            if p.state is None and p.country is None:\n                lib_bare.add(p.base)\n\n# bases that appear WITH a state in any Brazilian file\nstated = set()\nfor path, cols in [('data/kaggle/Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv', ('home_team','away_team')),\n                   ('data/kaggle/Brasileirao_Matches.csv', ('home_team','away_team')),\n                   ('data/kaggle/BR-Football-Dataset.csv', ('home','away')),\n                   ('data/kaggle/novo_campeonato_brasileiro.csv', ('Equipe_mandante','Equipe_visitante'))]:\n    with open(path, encoding='utf-8') as f:\n        for row in csv.DictReader(f):\n            for c in cols:\n                p = apply_aliases(parse_team_name(row[c]))\n                if p.state:\n                    stated.add(p.base)\n\nprint(\"Libertadores bare bases that ALSO exist as Brazilian state-suffixed clubs:\")\nfor b in sorted(lib_bare & stated):\n    print(\" \", b)\nEOF"},"output":"Libertadores bare bases that ALSO exist as Brazilian state-suffixed clubs:\n  botafogo\n  chapecoense\n  corinthians\n  cruzeiro\n  flamengo\n  fluminense\n  fortaleza\n  gremio\n  internacional\n  palmeiras\n  penarol\n  red bull bragantino\n  river plate\n  santos\n  sao paulo\n  vasco da gama\n","metadata":{"output":"Libertadores bare bases that ALSO exist as Brazilian state-suffixed clubs:\n  botafogo\n  chapecoense\n  corinthians\n  cruzeiro\n  flamengo\n  fluminense\n  fortaleza\n  gremio\n  internacional\n  palmeiras\n  penarol\n  red bull bragantino\n  river plate\n  santos\n  sao paulo\n  vasco da gama\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"source venv/bin/activate && python << 'EOF'\nimport csv\nfrom soccer_mcp.normalize import parse_team_name, apply_aliases\n\n# bare names in Libertadores (no state, no country)\nlib_bare = set()\nwith open('data/kaggle/Libertadores_Matches.csv', encoding='utf-8') as f:\n    for row in csv.DictReader(f):\n        for t in (row['home_team'], row['away_team']):\n            p = apply_aliases(parse_team_name(t))\n            if p.state is None and p.country is None:\n                lib_bare.add(p.base)\n\n# bases that appear WITH a state in any Brazilian file\nstated = set()\nfor path, cols in [('data/kaggle/Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv', ('home_team','away_team')),\n                   ('data/kaggle/Brasileirao_Matches.csv', ('home_team','away_team')),\n                   ('data/kaggle/BR-Football-Dataset.csv', ('home','away')),\n                   ('data/kaggle/novo_campeonato_brasileiro.csv', ('Equipe_mandante','Equipe_visitante'))]:\n    with open(path, encoding='utf-8') as f:\n        for row in csv.DictReader(f):\n            for c in cols:\n                p = apply_aliases(parse_team_name(row[c]))\n                if p.state:\n                    stated.add(p.base)\n\nprint(\"Libertadores bare bases that ALSO exist as Brazilian state-suffixed clubs:\")\nfor b in sorted(lib_bare & stated):\n    print(\" \", b)\nEOF","time":{"start":1788253561971,"end":1788253561975}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Found it — \"River Plate\" was resolved to \"river plate se\"! There's a \"River Plate - SE\" in the Brazilian Cup file (a tiny Sergipe club called \"River Plate-SE\"!) which merged with the famous Argentine \"River Plate\" because:\n1. Cup \"River Plate - SE\" → (\"river plate\", \"se\")\n2. Libertadores \"River Plate\" bare → base \"river plate\", states observed {se} → unique → auto-inherited \"se\" → \"river plate se\". WRONG — the famous River Plate (ARG) got merged with the Brazilian minnow.\n\nAlso match_count=92 mixes both clubs' matches (River Plate-SE played Copa do Brasil 2012 (2 matches), Libertadores River Plate has ~90). H2H/Grenals etc. unaffected (river plate doesn't play Brazilian league), but this is wrong.\n\nThis is the auto-inherit hazard: a foreign club sharing a base with an obscure Brazilian club. Similar risks: \"Botafogo\"? — hint rj ✓ fine. \"Internacional\"? hint rs ✓. Which other foreign clubs share bases with Brazilian ones?\n- \"River Plate\" vs River Plate-SE ✓ found.\n- \"Nacional\" (URU/PAR) — country-tagged → separate ids ✓; bare Brazilian \"Nacional\" → Nacional-AM... wait — is there a bare \"Nacional\" in Libertadores? Earlier list: \"Nacional (PAR)\", \"Nacional-URU\", \"Nacional (URU)\" — all tagged ✓. Brazilian \"Nacional - AM\" suffixed ✓. No bare. OK.\n- \"Guaraní (PAR)\" → \"guarani par\" ✓ vs Brazilian \"guarani sp\"/\"guarani ce\" ✓ distinct.\n- \"Colón\" vs? no.\n- \"Arsenal Sarandí\" vs \"Arsenal\"? no bare Arsenal.\n- \"Sporting Cristal\", \"Sport Boys\" vs \"Sport\"-PE — base \"sport boys\" ≠ \"sport\" ✓.\n- \"América de Cali\" → base \"america de cali\" ✓ distinct from \"america\".\n- \"Barcelona-EQU\" ✓ tagged.\n- \"Emelec\", \"LDU\", \"Melgar\" ✓.\n- \"Tigre\"/\"Tigres\" ✓ distinct bases.\n- \"Estudiantes\" (ARG) vs \"Estudiantes de Mérida\" (VEN) — distinct bases ✓.\n- \"Independiente\" (ARG) vs Brazilian \"Independente - PA\"! — \"Independiente\" ≠ \"Independente\" (different spelling: ie vs e) ✓ distinct.\n- \"Libertad\" vs \"Libertad-PAR\" → merged via country ✓ same club.\n- \"Olimpia-PAR\" ✓.\n- \"Junior de Barranquilla\" ✓.\n- \"Racing Club\" ✓ no Brazilian Racing.\n- \"San Lorenzo\" ✓.\n- \"Huracán\" ✓.\n- \"Lanús\" ✓.\n- \"Defensa y Justicia\", \"Defensor Sporting\" ✓.\n- \"Talleres\" ✓.\n- \"Vélez Sarsfield\" ✓.\n- \"Colo-Colo\" → \"colo colo\" ✓ no Brazilian.\n- \"Palestino\" ✓.\n- \"Universidad...\" ✓.\n- \"Always Ready\", \"The Strongest\", \"Bolívar\", \"San José\", \"Wilstermann\" ✓.\n- \"Peñarol\" (URU) — hmm! Cup has \"Penarol - AM\" (Brazilian Penarol-AM!) and Libertadores \"Peñarol\" (URU)! \"Peñarol\" bare → base \"penarol\" states {am} (from Penarol-AM cup) → auto-inherits am → \"penarol am\" — WRONG again! The Uruguayan Peñarol merged with Brazilian Penarol-AM.\n- \"Danubio\", \"Montevideo Wanderers\", \"Rentistas\" ✓.\n- \"Alianza Lima\" ✓.\n- \"Cerro Porteño\" ✓.\n\nSo two problematic merges: River Plate & Peñarol. Fix: add these to a `FOREIGN_BARE_NAMES` concept — when a bare base is a KNOWN foreign club name AND a Brazilian state-bearing club of the same base exists, keep them separate. Mechanism: FAMOUS_STATE_HINTS forces a state — but foreign clubs have no state. I need the opposite: a set of bases that should stay STATELESS (foreign) even when a Brazilian same-base club exists: {\"river plate\", \"penarol\"}.\n\nImplement: in `canonical_team_name`, after computing states: if base in FOREIGN_BARE_NAMES → return stateless. And in the registry auto-derivation, stateless foreign entities get their own id (base alone) — \"river plate\" ✓ separate from \"river plate se\" ✓.\n\nBut wait — there's an ordering subtlety: `register()` decides ids per raw name. Cup \"River Plate - SE\" → (\"river plate\",\"se\") → id \"river plate se\" ✓. Libertadores \"River Plate\" → base \"river plate\" in FOREIGN_BARE_NAMES → stays stateless → id \"river plate\" ✓. \n\nFIFA \"River Plate\"? FIFA has River Plate (ARG) → \"river plate\" stateless ✓ same id as Libertadores ✓.\n\nAlso — the resolution: query \"River Plate\" → base river plate → candidates: \"river plate\" (stateless, foreign) and \"river plate se\" (Brazilian) → my resolve logic: stateless parsed → candidates by base → 2 → brazilian-only? \"river plate\" entity has country None (is_brazilian True by my property — wrong!). Hmm — `is_brazilian` = country is None. Stateless foreign entities have country None → considered \"brazilian\". That breaks the \"unique Brazilian wins\" logic but doesn't crash: 2 candidates → both \"brazilian\" → ambiguous error! Query \"River Plate\" would fail with ambiguity — bad.\n\nBetter: add `country: \"arg\"`? I can't know the country reliably. Alternative: mark these entities as foreign via the FOREIGN_BARE_NAMES set: TeamEntity gets `is_foreign` flag? Simplest: extend the entity with a `foreign: bool` field set when the base is in FOREIGN_BARE_NAMES... but TeamEntity is a dataclass in model.py — I can add a field `foreign: bool = False` and registry sets it. Then `is_brazilian` = not foreign and country is None. And in resolve: candidates filtering — unique non-foreign Brazilian wins... but with hints already handling famous Brazilian clubs, \"River Plate\" query → candidates: river plate (foreign), river plate se (Brazilian, tiny) → unique Brazilian → river plate se wins?! Wrong — user asking \"River Plate\" usually means the Argentine club in a Libertadores context...\n\nHmm. Pragmatics: in a Brazilian-soccer dataset, how do the two compare? river plate (Libertadores, 92 matches) vs river plate se (2 CdB matches). Match-count ranking favors the famous one. My resolve for ambiguous case (all brazilian per old logic) — with the foreign flag, let me use: prefer entity with more matches when bases collide and one is foreign-flagged? Actually my `_fuzzy` has a dominance rule; the exact-base ambiguous path doesn't. Let me refine resolve: for base-collision ambiguity: if exactly one candidate is NOT foreign-flagged → hmm no...\n\nSimplest robust rule: in the base-collision case, sort candidates by match_count and pick the dominant one if it has ≥ 3× the matches of the runner-up AND ≥ 10 matches; else ambiguous error. For \"river plate\": 92 vs 2 → dominant → river plate (famous) ✓. For \"atletico\": MG 700+ vs PR 700+... ratio < 3 → ambiguous ✓. For \"america\": MG ~600 vs RN ~100? ratio maybe > 3... hmm — that would auto-pick América-MG. Is that bad? It'd match the \"famous hint\" philosophy. But earlier I decided América should be ambiguous (no hint). With dominance rule it might auto-resolve. Let me check counts: america mg in Serie A 2003-2023 many seasons ~700 matches; america rn Serie B/C ~200 matches. Ratio 3.5 → dominant → america mg. Hmm — I could live with that... but it makes behavior data-dependent and harder to explain. \n\nAlternative cleaner rule: FOREIGN_BARE_NAMES = {\"river plate\", \"penarol\"} — these bases keep their stateless (foreign) entity. In resolve, for base collisions: prefer NON-foreign unique... no wait — that picks river plate se.\n\nOK here's the cleanest: make FOREIGN_BARE_NAMES a RESOLUTION hint too — when a query's base is in FOREIGN_BARE_NAMES and the query has no state, prefer the stateless entity. I.e., FOREIGN_BARE_NAMES acts like FAMOUS_STATE_HINTS but pointing to \"stateless foreign id\". Implement in resolve: after candidates: if parsed.stateless and parsed.base in FOREIGN_BARE_NAMES and base (stateless id) in entities → use it (alternatives = the Brazilian ones). And in canonical_team_name (registration path): if base in FOREIGN_BARE_NAMES → return stateless parsed (no state inheritance).\n\nLet me implement:\n1. normalize.py: add `FOREIGN_BARE_NAMES: set[str] = {\"river plate\", \"penarol\"}`; in `canonical_team_name`: if parsed.state is None and parsed.country is None and parsed.base in FOREIGN_BARE_NAMES → return parsed as-is (skip auto-inherit + hints).\n   Careful: \"River Plate-SE\" query (with state) → parsed (\"river plate\",\"se\") → state present → skips the FOREIGN check (state not None) → id \"river plate se\" ✓.\n2. Registry: nothing special — entities are per id ✓. `is_brazilian` stays country-is-None (foreign \"river plate\" counts as brazilian — only used for candidate filtering; the FOREIGN_BARE_NAMES resolution in resolve bypasses).\n   In resolve: in the stateless-candidates branch, before the brazilian-unique logic: if parsed.base in FOREIGN_BARE_NAMES and parsed.base in self.entities → return that entity with alternatives. \n   \n   Hmm — resolve currently calls canonical_team_name WITH registry_states which auto-inherits... with the FOREIGN check first, \"River Plate\" query → parsed stateless base \"river plate\" → then resolve: since parsed.state is None and country None → candidates = _by_base → [river plate, river plate se] → len>1 → FOREIGN check → pick \"river plate\" ✓.\n   \n   Also — do I want the same in the Nickname path? No.\n\n3. Also fix the ALREADY-BUILT registry data: after fix, \"River Plate\" (Libertadores) → \"river plate\" stateless; Cup \"River Plate - SE\" → \"river plate se\" ✓. The entity display: \"River Plate\" ✓. match_count: river plate = 92 (Libertadores) + FIFA club (0 matches); river plate se = 2 ✓.\n\nLet me also scan for OTHER foreign/Brazilian base collisions I may have missed by writing a quick check script: after loading, find entities whose variants include both a Libertadores-only name and a Cup state-suffixed name... simpler: find ids where state is not None but the entity has matches in libertadores from a BARE variant. Actually — let me check: which Libertadores bare team names share a base with a Brazilian state-bearing club? Compute directly.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05c37e376001QVD1rgu0WZOGAj","sessionID":"ses_fa40f0460ffeYpKGnJPSwDwXVy","messageID":"msg_05c3661e3001EpEFlL2v5Bv9Q7"}}
