 club of\n #: the same base (River Plate-SE, Peñarol-AM).  These must NOT inherit a\n #: Brazilian state; they stay stateless so the famous foreign club and the\n #: small Brazilian club remain separate entities.\n-FOREIGN_BARE_NAMES: {\"river plate\", \"penarol\"}\n+FOREIGN_BARE_NAMES = {\"river plate\", \"penarol\"}\n \n #: Popular nicknames accepted as queries (not present in the raw data).\n NICKNAMES: dict[str, str] = {\n     \"fla\": \"flamengo rj\",\n","additions":1,"deletions":1},"truncated":false},"title":"soccer_mcp/normalize.py","time":{"start":1788253589717,"end":1788253589719}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Oops — I wrote `FOREIGN_BARE_NAMES: {\"river plate\", \"penarol\"}` — that's an annotated EXPRESSION, not a set! `FOREGN_BARE_NAMES: {\"river plate\", \"penarol\"}` — the annotation must be a type: `FOREIGN_BARE_NAMES: set[str] = {\"river plate\", \"penarol\"}`. Python would actually evaluate `{\"river plate\", \"penarol\"}` as an annotation (annotations can be any expression) and FOREIGN_BARE_NAMES would be UNDEFINED. Fix it.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05c3850c6001uxM1v9RgFnBm08","sessionID":"ses_fa40f0460ffeYpKGnJPSwDwXVy","messageID":"msg_05c383153001mqOnoHca5UC1Ci"}}
