text":"Two bugs:\n\n1. **Boavista parenthetical not dropped!** The regex `_RE_TRAILING_PAREN = re.compile(r\"\\(([^)]*)\\)\\s*$\")` requires the paren at the END of the string — but \"Boavista Sport Club (antigo Esporte Clube Barreira) - RJ\" has the paren in the MIDDLE (state suffix after it). Order in parse_team_name: paren first (fails — not at end), then state suffix (matches \" - RJ\" → strips → \"Boavista Sport Club (antigo Esporte Clube Barreira)\"), then _clean_base keeps the paren content (only `re.sub(r\"[^\\w\\s]\", \" \", ...)` removes parens themselves but keeps words). Need: after stripping state/country, ALSO check for a trailing parenthetical on the remaining text, and if it's not a state/country marker, drop it. Restructure: \n   1. strip trailing state suffix or country tag (loop twice? \" - RJ\" then paren check).\n   Actually simplest: run the paren check AFTER suffix extraction, repeatedly: first extract state/country suffix, then if text ends with ')', extract paren: if state-name/uf/country → use; else drop; then try state suffix again (e.g. \"Something (XX) - YY\"?). Rare. I'll do: try state/country suffix; then try paren (dropping or promoting); then try state suffix again once.\n\n2. **Santos FWD = 0!** Santos club has 20 players — but no forwards?? Let me look at Santos players' positions. In FIFA 19, Brazilian club squads are GENERIC (fake players). Wait — Santos players: let me list them. Maybe their positions are like \"ST\" but my filter... `if position_key: if player.position != position_key and player.position_group != group: continue` — for position_key=\"FWD\", player.position=\"ST\": \"ST\" != \"FWD\" → True; player.position_group == \"FWD\" == group → `!= group` → False → `True and False` → False → don't skip. Should work! Unless Santos players have empty positions or GK-ish codes. Let me print Santos squad.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05c3d58c2001t3fXt9beFl4S4z","sessionID":"ses_fa40f0460ffeYpKGnJPSwDwXVy","messa